{"parentUuid":"8d47c593-55bf-4957-8d98-fc8eda9774e5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013QyemvFox2SWbTEGzKoTQK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review: Supertraining Ch 6a (6.1-6.3), 28 entries\n2\t\n3\tReviewer: Claude Opus (independent falsification pass). Date: 2026-10-03.\n4\tSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 324-347 (printed 313-336). Text pulled with PyMuPDF; every figure and table checked against 200 dpi renders in `staging/_renders/r6a_p*.png`. I opened the images for PDF 330 (Fig 6.1), 332 (Ryun Fig 6.2), 333 (second Fig 6.2, Figs 6.3-6.5), 334 (Fig 6.6, worked example), 335 (Fig 6.7, Table 6.1), 336 (notations, Table 6.2), 341 (Table 6.5) and 343 (order/causal analysis, Chernyak).\n5\tBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 104, 105, 106, 107, 108, 142, 179, 180, 181, 183, 185, 186, 187. All quotes are verbatim and all tags are correct. Line 106 (undulating loading) is quoted exactly with tag \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)\".\n6\tWikilinks: all `[[...]]` targets in the 28 files resolve to files in the vault library or the staging library. None are broken.\n7\t\n8\t## Per-entry verdicts\n9\t\n10\t| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n11\t|---|---|---|---|\n12\t| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson | FIX | The entry says: \"The authors themselves warn **on the next page** that this history 'may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation'\". That warning is on PDF 325 / p.314, the same page as Dyson's five phases (the \"Ways of Organising Training\" heading is on PDF 325). Everything else checked: 10 months (PDF 324); Kotov 2 and 4 months; Pikhala; Grantyn; Ozolin; Letunov (PDF 325); Dyson 5 mo / 1 mo / 6 / 6 / 6 wk (PDF 325). | Replace \"on the next page\" with \"on the same page\". |\n13\t| claim--seasonal-trainability-hettinger-muller-ultraviolet | GO | checked: the winter low and the summer/autumn high, the \"sharp drop at the onset of winter\", Hettinger 1961, and the artificial UV result, which runs across PDF 324-325 / p.313-314. The hedge \"seemed to coincide\" is kept. `supported` is acceptable for a single-lab finding. | none |\n14\t| framework--sport-alone-vs-sport-plus-supplementary-training-and-adjunct-modes | GO | checked: the two categories (PDF 325); 50-60% versus 20-30%; the gymnastics exception; the three adjunct modes (PDF 326 / p.315); baseline line 104 verbatim. The Example is labelled Inference. | none |\n15\t| heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation | GO | checked: the \"false impression\" quote (PDF 325-326); \"It is inadequate to train conservatively ...\" (PDF 326); the contingency list; the gym-lifter link (PDF 329); baseline line 185. | Optional: add `[[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]]` (6.14.4, same book) to Related. |\n16\t| claim--hettinger-four-features-that-stimulate-strength-gains | GO | checked: all four points verbatim (PDF 325 / p.314); \"modified and extended by more recent research\". The Ch1 threshold entry does say \"one-third\" (1953) and \"80-95%\", so the comparison holds. Baseline line 108 verbatim. | none |\n17\t| claim--heavy-medium-light-alternation-beats-monotonic-increasing-loading | GO | checked: the Fry 1994a/b sentence and its hedge \"would appear to\" (PDF 326); the Kraemer and Hakkinen citations; the PDF 329 repeat. The Fig 6.4 caption and the L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S labels match the image (PDF 333). Baseline line 106 verbatim. The Verkhoshansky \"word play\" point is correctly given \"as reported by Siff\". | none |\n18\t| framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap | GO | checked: definition; the units; 5-10 days / 1-4 months / 10-12 months; minimum two workout types; Bulgarian >5 sessions; the meso/macro swap (PDF 327 / p.316); \"commonly a week long\" (PDF 337); baseline line 179. | Optional dedup link: `[[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]`. |\n19\t| claim--conversion-phase-is-a-misnomer | GO | checked: the whole quote (PDF 327). The Example quote \"against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block\" is real, at PDF 304 (speed-strength and complex-technique sequence, step 3). Baseline line 180 verbatim. | none |\n20\t| framework--matveyev-microcycle-and-mesocycle-types-and-sequences | GO | checked: the five microcycle types (PDF 327); the four sample mesocycles; the six mesocycle types; the endurance and speed-strength sequences (PDF 328); baseline lines 186 and 187. The tension between \"ordinary\" and alternation is handled correctly as Inference. | none |\n21\t| heuristic--shock-microcycle-limits | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 327-328), including the 3-4 per year and one double shock per year; baseline line 142 verbatim. | none |\n22\t| heuristic--gpp-and-spp-form-one-unit-curtail-gpp-for-advanced-athletes | GO | checked: Bondarchuk 1979; GPP contents; the hypertrophy-phase strength-deficit decision; the curtailment for advanced athletes; GPP inside SPP (PDF 328, where \"SPF\" is an OCR error for SPP); the transition warning (PDF 329); baseline line 105 and its \"Not claimed in the interview\" note. | none |\n23\t| heuristic--in-season-stabilisation-twice-weekly-30-minutes | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 329 / p.318). Baseline line 105 is quoted correctly with both caveats. The Example is labelled Inference. | none |\n24\t| heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 329). The Inference \"15% vs 37% in Fig 6.3A, about 41%\" matches the image (PDF 333: 15, 23, 37, 25); 15/37 = 40.5%. Baseline line 106. | none |\n25\t| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability | FIX | The entry says \"the **third** bullet in the source list ('the premature introduction of skills')\". On PDF 329 / p.318 the bullets are, in order: (1) \"eliminating any factors from training which disrupt learning of technique\", (2) \"the premature introduction of skills ...\", (3) \"the ability to master mental control ...\". It is the second bullet. Everything else checked: 70-80%; Matveyev 1977; gym lifters; feedforward and feedback (PDF 330). | Change \"the third bullet\" to \"the second bullet\". |\n26\t| framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model | GO | checked: the Fig 6.1 image (PDF 330). Volume falls and intensity rises through the preparation phase and TF1, and they cross inside TF1; the technique curve rises to a plateau in the competition phase. All 8 items (PDF 330-331) and Medvedev 1986 checked. The item 5 voice note is correct. The Evans link is a fair cross-reference. | none |\n27\t| principle--sporting-form-cannot-be-held-permanently-matveyev | GO | checked: the Ryun Fig 6.2 image (PDF 332). The tallest bars, about 170 km, are opening microcycle 1 and INTERM microcycle 1; the opening block has a short second bar about 10-15 km tall; the late competitive bars are about 35, 45, 40 and 22 km; the result points run about 97-100% with WR dots. The two figures captioned \"Figure 6.2\" are confirmed (PDF 332 and 333). The question and answer quotes are verbatim (PDF 331). | none |\n28\t| framework--six-broad-periodisation-schemes | GO | checked: all six schemes and their numbers (PDF 332-333); the \"system 4\" sentence (PDF 334), correctly flagged as probable misnumbering; the Issurin 2016 caveat is aimed at the right target. | none |\n29\t| case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions | GO | checked against the images (PDF 333-334): Fig 6.2A 42/23/35; B 54/30/16; Fig 6.3A 15/23/37/25; B 13/21/30/36; Fig 6.4 L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S; Fig 6.5 L,M,L,S,M,L,M,L, whose caption does compare it with Fig 6.3; Fig 6.6 L,L,S,M,L,L,S,M. All cells correct. `untested` is appropriate. | none |\n30\t| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | FIX | The flagged slip is real: on PDF 334 / p.323, 3 x 3 at 75 kg = 75/120 = 62.5%, so the Inference is correct. But the entry also says \"the worked example drops a 50% set as a warm-up, **which follows the 70% rule**.\" It does not. The example keeps the 80 kg x 8 set, which is 80/120 = **66.7% of 1RM**, below the \"less than about 70% of 1RM\" warm-up exclusion printed in the paragraph just above. Under Chernyak's >40% rule, the 60 kg (50%) set would count. The worked example follows neither stated rule. | Replace with: \"the worked example drops the 60 kg (50%) set but keeps the 80 kg set (66.7% of 1RM), so it follows neither the 70% warm-up rule nor Chernyak's 40% rule (PDF 334 / p.323). Record which rule a log uses.\" |\n31\t| framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording | GO | checked: the five zones (PDF 335 text). Table 6.1 column I is headed \"60%-\" in the image, so the flagged inconsistency is real and correctly described. Fig 6.7: A peaks at zone III, about 40; B is skewed low; C is skewed high (by eye). The near-miss rule and the notation (superscripts are set counts, 10(60)^1, 8(70)^1, 6(80)^2) checked on PDF 336. | Optional: note that Fig 6.7's own axis labels zone I \"50+\", which agrees with the text and not with Table 6.1. |\n32\t| claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification | GO | checked: all cited Table 6.2 cells against the image (PDF 336): 52 kg 51.0/63.5/70.0/78.0/80.0; 75 kg 70.0/92.0/99.0/111.0/116.0; 100 kg 82.0/101.0/113.0/125.0/132.0; +110 kg 86.0/109.0/120.0/130.0/147.0. The \"optimal\" versus \"upper limits\" tension is real. Dvorkin bands 50-70 / 60-90 / 80-90%. | none |\n33\t| heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions | GO | checked: Russian versus Bulgarian 1RM, novice pre-testing, 5RM/6RM (PDF 337); Fig 6.8 sequence; the \"work backwards\" quote (PDF 337); Table 6.3 means; \"guidelines, not scientific computations\"; the modular day (PDF 338); baseline lines 183, 107, 185. | Optional dedup links: `[[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]]` (6.15), `[[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]]` (6.14.5), `[[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]`. |\n34\t| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | FIX | All quotes and numbers check (PDF 339-340: RPE scales 1-3, 1-10 and 1-5; \"identify any decrease\"; Wiener 1948; Bulgarian -10 kg / +5 kg; minor exercises; manual labourers). The voice Inference overreaches. PDF 339-340 name neither author in the third person and do not cite Siff for the method (Siff 1990 is cited only for order and causal analysis on PDF 342). \"This textbook introduced\" (PDF 345) shows that Siff, writing 6.3, credits *the book*, not himself. The third-person test therefore does not establish Siff here. | Replace the voice sentence with: \"Voice: pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author. Section 6.3, written in Siff's voice, refers back to it as what 'this textbook introduced' (PDF 345 / p.334). Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" |\n35\t| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | FIX | Novik protocol (PDF 340) and PWC-170 protocol and formula (PDF 341) checked; all 11 Table 6.5 rows match the image. Overreach: the entry lists \"pulls 0%\" as part of the table's inconsistency with \"+70%\". The text applies the 70% only \"in each case **where there is significant eccentric action**\" (PDF 341), so 0% for the clean and snatch pulls fits that qualifier. The real inconsistencies are the ratios above or between: bench +100%, squat clean about +88%, split clean +40%, split snatch about +33%, good morning about +49%, jerk about +11%, power clean about +6%. | Reword to: \"...the table's ratios differ: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit, but bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it, and split clean, split snatch, good morning, jerk and power clean (about +6% to +49%) fall between; the pulls at 0% fit the 'significant eccentric action' qualifier.\" |\n36\t| framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation | GO | checked: the 5 HR modes and 80% max for rowers, BP (PDF 341); Shishkin 1989, Siff 1977, 5-6 h; the Ceff formula and Spiridonova and Kuznetsova 1986; Rodionov; the palpation sites (PDF 342). The Inference arithmetic is correct (300/180 = 1.67; 300/190 = 1.58). `untested` fits. | none |\n37\t| framework--order-and-causal-analysis-for-judging-training-schemes | GO | checked: Siff 1990 (PDF 342); all four causal methods with their limitations; \"Medeleev 1869\" as printed (PDF 343 image). The Mill's-methods remark is labelled Inference. | none |\n38\t| claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations | GO | checked against the image (PDF 343 / p.332): 12-15, 9-10, 6-7, 3-4, 2-3, 3-4 and 1-2 weeks. They sum to 36 (minimum) and 45 (maximum), so \"about 36-45\" is correct. Contradiction (a) is real: the opening says the most frequent weeks include \"high volume and high intensity\", while rec. 3 gives large/high only 2-3 weeks. Contradiction (b) is real: rec. 1 versus rec. 2 on medium/medium. `contested` is justified as a same-page contradiction. Chernyak is cited as \"1978\" on PDF 334 and as \"et al, 1979\" here; the entry uses the correct form for each page. | none |\n39\t| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | The voice is correct: PDF 344-345 name Verkhoshansky in the third person throughout and say \"this textbook introduced\" and \"this book paid special attention\". Siff's framing and Verkhoshansky's points are not mixed up, and the Zheljazkov quotes are verbatim (PDF 345-346). Both sides are on the cited pages. The other library sources are named correctly (Issurin 2016, Harries, Afonso, Evans); no Bompa entry in the vault takes a side, so none is missing. **Misattribution:** \"British runners declined after adopting 'Matveyev's block scheme' widely after 1980 (citing Horwill 1992, 1995; Tschiene 1991, 1992)\". On PDF 344, Horwill is cited for condemning \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation\". Tschiene is cited separately, for the claim that \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\". The British-decline claim is Verkhoshansky's own statement. | Split the citations: \"British runners declined after adopting 'Matveyev's block scheme' widely after 1980 (Verkhoshansky's own claim); he cites Horwill (1992, 1995) condemning 'the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation', and Tschiene (1991, 1992) concluding that periodisation 'has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965' (PDF 344 / p.333).\" Optional: add Verkhoshansky's point that periodisation reflects Soviet five-year-plan thinking, and Zheljazkov's partial agreement with it (PDF 345). |\n40\t\n41\t## Duplicate check\n42\t\n43\t- Vault and staging grep for: Rule of Sixty, shock microcycle, cybernetic, Chernyak, Zheljazkov, conversion phase, PWC, coefficient of effectiveness, order analysis, stabilisation phase, Ryun, Kotov/Dyson/Pikhala, reliability of technique. No existing entry restates any in-scope entry.\n44\t- Within the batch: the 60% rule entry and the heavy/medium/light entry both quote the PDF 329 paragraph. The angles are distinct (a number versus the alternation claim), and the two cross-link. The Vorobyev case study and the heavy/medium/light entry share Fig 6.4 as an example; that is acceptable. None needs merging.\n45\t- Against Ch6b-d (out of scope): the backwards-plan entry overlaps in topic with 6.15's eight-step sequence and 6.14.5's calendar rules, but the angle is different (classical bar-chart planning versus Verkhoshansky's programming). Link them; do not merge. The event-specific entry overlaps with 6.14.4's technical-mastery principle; again, link only.\n46\t- Against Bompa, Issurin and Evans: the training-cycle and backwards-plan entries sit beside Evans's `bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` and `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`. The book-specific details (meso/macro swap, 5RM estimation, Fig 6.8) are new angles; add the links.\n47\t\n48\t## Missing (important ideas with no entry)\n49\t\n50\t1. **Chronobiology and periodisation** (PDF 346-347 / p.335-336). The circadian system and the SCN entrained by the retina; exercise and emotional arousal shift rhythms through intergeniculate and raphe inputs; this is offered as possible future corroboration of periodisation. It is the section's only physiological argument for cycling.\n51\t2. **Verkhoshansky's \"communism\" argument and Zheljazkov's partial concession** (PDF 345 / p.334). Periodisation's cyclicity was modelled on Soviet five-year plans, and its precise quantities were meant to remove subjectivity. Zheljazkov agrees that society shapes training but says the effect is not always negative. It could be a sentence in the controversy entry.\n52\t3. **Tschiene's claim** that periodisation is unchanged since Matveyev's 1965 formalisation, and that coaching doctrine has not advanced (PDF 344). It is only lost through the citation slip above.\n53\t4. **\"The organisation of training is as much a matter of art, trial-and-error, and intuition\"** and the fractal/chaotic-systems rationale (PDF 344). It is quoted inside the controversy entry; a standalone principle could reconcile it with 6.17's \"programming is an art\" entry. Low priority.\n54\t5. **Matveyev's critique of the \"notorious peaks\" versus season-long demand** is covered. Nothing else of weight is uncaptured; 6.1-6.3 is otherwise densely covered.\n55\t\n56\t## Reciprocal link suggestions\n57\t\n58\t- `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice` <-> `heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation`\n59\t- `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training` and `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` <-> `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions`\n60\t- `elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` <-> `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`\n61\t- `principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm` <-> `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov`\n62\t\n63\t## Counts\n64\t\n65\tGO 22, FIX 6, NO-GO 0.\n66\t\n67\t## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n68\t\n69\tRequired fixes:\n70\t1. early-history: \"on the next page\" becomes \"on the same page\" (PDF 325).\n71\t2. progress-skills: \"third bullet\" becomes \"second bullet\" (PDF 329).\n72\t3. volume-intensity calculation: the worked example keeps an 80 kg set (66.7% of 1RM), so it does not follow the 70% rule; reword (PDF 334).\n73\t4. cybernetic periodisation: soften the Siff attribution; the page names neither author (PDF 339-340, 345).\n74\t5. heart-rate tests: reword the Table 6.5 inconsistency; the pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier (PDF 341).\n75\t6. periodisation controversy: separate the Horwill and Tschiene citations from the British-decline claim and add Tschiene's actual point (PDF 344).\n76\t# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.1-6.3 (Ch6a), 28 entries\n77\t\n78\tFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 6 FIX rows, optional Related links on 4 GO rows, 5 missing ideas).\n79\tSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 324-347 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6a_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 325, 329, 334, 339, 340, 341, 344, 345, 346 and 347 to `staging/_renders/f6a_p<N>.png` at 200 dpi. I opened PDF 325 (Dyson's phases and the \"may create the impression\" warning under \"Ways of Organising Training\"), 329 (the three bullets after \"zone-by-zone mastering\"), 334 (Exercise A worked example, the 40% and 70% rules), 340 (cybernetic periodisation, no author named), 341 (Table 6.5 and the \"significant eccentric action\" sentence), 344 (Horwill, British decline, Tschiene), 345 (\"this textbook introduced\", the five-year-plan passage, Zheljazkov's partial agreement) and 346 (chronobiology). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\n80\tScope: the 28 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt`. I edited the 6 flagged entries, plus 3 GO entries for the optional Related links. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (28 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6b, 6c or 6d file, or the vault.\n81\t\n82\t| file | what changed |\n83\t|---|---|\n84\t| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md | **Fix 1.** \"warn on the next page\" is now \"warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\". Verified on PDF 325 / p.314: the Dyson list, the \"Ways of Organising Training\" heading and the \"may create the impression ... far from the truth\" sentence are all on that page. |\n85\t| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md | **Fix 2.** \"the third bullet\" is now \"the second bullet in the source list (\"the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification\", PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318)\". Verified on the image: the bullets are (1) eliminating disruptive factors, (2) premature introduction of skills, (3) mental control. |\n86\t| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md | **Fix 3.** \"drops a 50% set as a warm-up, which follows the 70% rule\" is replaced: the example drops the 60 kg (50%) set but keeps the 80 kg set (66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM). \"Inference from the printed numbers: the example therefore follows neither the 70% warm-up rule (which would also drop the 80 kg set) nor Chernyak's 40% rule (which would keep the 60 kg set)\", with the PDF 334 / p.323 locator. \"Record which rule a log uses\" is kept. The arithmetic is labelled Inference because it is the fixer's, not the authors'. |\n87\t| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md | **Fix 4.** The \"Voice, Inference: ... read as Siff's own contribution\" sentence is replaced. Now: pages 339-340 name neither author and address the reader as \"you\"; Section 6.3, in Siff's voice, refers back to \"methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" that \"this textbook introduced\" and to \"the section on Cybernetic Periodisation\" (PDF 345 / p.334, verified on the image); \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" |\n88\t| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md | **Fix 5.** The Table 6.5 sentence now gives all eleven ratios, recomputed from the image of PDF 341 / p.330: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit the 70%; bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it; split clean about +40%, split snatch about +33%, deep good morning about +49%, jerk about +11% and power clean about +6% fall between; the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier. \"pulls 0%\" is no longer listed as an inconsistency. |\n89\t| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md | **Fix 6.** The British-decline claim is now \"(Verkhoshansky's own claim)\". Horwill (1992, 1995) is cited for \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation as used by some runners in different countries\". Tschiene (1991, 1992) is cited for \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\" and \"many coach's doctrines have not advanced\" (PDF 344 / p.333, verified on the image). The \"word play\" sentence now carries its own locator, PDF 345 / p.334. **Missing idea 2** added to the Verkhoshansky paragraph (the communism / \"five year plans\" / \"subjectivity and emotiveness\" passage) and to the Zheljazkov paragraph (his partial agreement, quoted), both PDF 345 / p.334. **Missing idea 3** (Tschiene) is covered by the fix. **Missing idea 1** added as a paragraph, \"Chronobiology, the section's closing argument\" (PDF 346-347 / p.335-336), with \"Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles\" and a labelled Inference. The What it is range is now PDF 344-347 / printed 333-336. **Accuracy fix beyond the review:** the Example said British \"middle-distance runners won Olympic gold before, not after\" adopting the scheme. PDF 344 does not say that: \"middle distances\" refers to Soviet runners, and the British claim is \"did not gain gold medals on Olympic games over the last 30 years, but produced great achievements before they used such concepts\". The Example now quotes that sentence. **Optional link** added: [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]]. |\n90\t| heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation.md | Optional link: [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]] added to Related. |\n91\t| framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap.md | Optional link: [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]] added to Related. |\n92\t| heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md | Optional links: [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]] added to Related. |\n93\t\n94\tAll new link targets exist today: the four Supertraining targets in the staging book folder, and the two Evans targets in both the staging library and the vault.\n95\t\n96\t## Missing ideas: placement\n97\t\n98\t| # | idea | placement |\n99\t|---|---|---|\n100\t| 1 | Chronobiology and periodisation (PDF 346-347) | Folded into the controversy entry as a paragraph. It is the closing subsection of 6.3 and offers itself as \"future corroboration\" in the same debate. The paragraph says `untested`, and why. The entry-level `contested` stays, because it describes the Verkhoshansky-Zheljazkov dispute. No new entry: the passage is a pointer to a research field with no training data, not a substantive idea that needs its own entry. |\n101\t| 2 | Five-year-plan argument and Zheljazkov's partial agreement (PDF 345) | Folded into the controversy entry's Verkhoshansky and Zheljazkov paragraphs, quoted. |\n102\t| 3 | Tschiene's claim (PDF 344) | Covered by fix 6. |\n103\t| 4 | \"art, trial-and-error, and intuition\" and the fractal rationale (PDF 344) | Already quoted in the controversy entry's Siff's-framing paragraph. The review calls it low priority. I added the reciprocal link to the 6.17 \"programming is an art\" principle instead of a new entry. |\n104\t| 5 | Matveyev's \"notorious peaks\" critique | The review says it is already covered; confirmed in the Verkhoshansky paragraph. |\n105\t\n106\tNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` is unchanged.\n107\t\n108\t## Disagreements with the review\n109\t\n110\tNone of the six fixes was wrong. Every claim held on the page image. Notes on how they were applied:\n111\t1. **Fix 3 and fix 5 are labelled Inference.** The review's replacement text reads as plain statements, but the percentages are the fixer's arithmetic, not printed values, so the entry labels them \"Inference from the printed numbers\" / \"Inference from the printed values\".\n112\t2. **Fix 4 quote widened.** The review quotes only \"this textbook introduced\". The entry now also quotes 6.3's explicit pointer \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" on the same page, which is the stronger evidence that 6.3 refers back to this method.\n113\t3. **Not applied: the optional Fig 6.7 axis note** on `framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording.md` (zone I labelled \"50+\"). The task limited optional work to Related links and missing ideas. It remains open for the coordinator.\n114\t\n115\t## For the coordinator\n116\t\n117\t- Reciprocal links (6b-6d files, not edited by me) the review suggests: `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice` should link back to [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]]. `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training` and `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` should link back to [[heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions]]. `principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm` should link back to [[claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov]]. The Evans entries are outside this book.\n118\t- Confidence tallies are unchanged.\n119\t\n120\t## Checks\n121\t\n122\tGrep proof, run from the book folder:\n123\t\n124\t```\n125\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:(Verkhoshansky's own claim)\n126\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:He cites Horwill (1992, 1995)\n127\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation\n128\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:five year plans\n129\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:22:partly agrees\n130\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:24:Chronobiology, the section's closing argument\n131\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:24:Confidence for this part: `untested`\n132\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:32:did not gain gold medals on Olympic games\n133\tclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:34:programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm\n134\tframework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md:24:pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author\n135\tframework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md:24:treat it as the authors'\n136\tframework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md:30:warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\n137\tframework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap.md:34:bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types\n138\tframework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md:30:keeps the 80 kg set, which is 66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM\n139\tframework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md:30:follows neither the 70% warm-up rule\n140\theuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation.md:26:technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice\n141\theuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training\n142\theuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates\n143\theuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan\n144\theuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md:24:bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it\n145\theuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md:24:the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier\n146\theuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md:22:the second bullet in the source list\n147\t```\n148\t\n149\tOld phrases (\"on the next page\", \"the third bullet\", \"which follows the 70% rule\", \"read as Siff's own contribution\", \"pulls 0%)\", \"(citing Horwill\", \"won Olympic gold before\"): scoped files still holding any of them: none.\n150\t\n151\tMechanical greps over all 28 scoped files:\n152\t\n153\t```\n154\t== em dashes not in section headers:\n155\t(end)\n156\t== wrapped wikilinks:\n157\t(end)\n158\t== review-history words:\n159\t(end)\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\tLint, run from the Flint root:\n163\t\n164\t```\n165\t$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\n166\tsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\n167\tTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n168\t```\n169\t\n170\tNo 6a file has a lint line. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.\n171\t"}]},"uuid":"bd4e1c1c-7a83-49e8-a3e7-a097830a7c63","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:13.038Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/baed41m93.txt","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 6a (6.1-6.3), 28 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent falsification pass). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 324-347 (printed 313-336). Text pulled with PyMuPDF; every figure and table checked against 200 dpi renders in `staging/_renders/r6a_p*.png`. I opened the images for PDF 330 (Fig 6.1), 332 (Ryun Fig 6.2), 333 (second Fig 6.2, Figs 6.3-6.5), 334 (Fig 6.6, worked example), 335 (Fig 6.7, Table 6.1), 336 (notations, Table 6.2), 341 (Table 6.5) and 343 (order/causal analysis, Chernyak).\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 104, 105, 106, 107, 108, 142, 179, 180, 181, 183, 185, 186, 187. All quotes are verbatim and all tags are correct. Line 106 (undulating loading) is quoted exactly with tag \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)\".\nWikilinks: all `[[...]]` targets in the 28 files resolve to files in the vault library or the staging library. None are broken.\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson | FIX | The entry says: \"The authors themselves warn **on the next page** that this history 'may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation'\". That warning is on PDF 325 / p.314, the same page as Dyson's five phases (the \"Ways of Organising Training\" heading is on PDF 325). Everything else checked: 10 months (PDF 324); Kotov 2 and 4 months; Pikhala; Grantyn; Ozolin; Letunov (PDF 325); Dyson 5 mo / 1 mo / 6 / 6 / 6 wk (PDF 325). | Replace \"on the next page\" with \"on the same page\". |\n| claim--seasonal-trainability-hettinger-muller-ultraviolet | GO | checked: the winter low and the summer/autumn high, the \"sharp drop at the onset of winter\", Hettinger 1961, and the artificial UV result, which runs across PDF 324-325 / p.313-314. The hedge \"seemed to coincide\" is kept. `supported` is acceptable for a single-lab finding. | none |\n| framework--sport-alone-vs-sport-plus-supplementary-training-and-adjunct-modes | GO | checked: the two categories (PDF 325); 50-60% versus 20-30%; the gymnastics exception; the three adjunct modes (PDF 326 / p.315); baseline line 104 verbatim. The Example is labelled Inference. | none |\n| heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation | GO | checked: the \"false impression\" quote (PDF 325-326); \"It is inadequate to train conservatively ...\" (PDF 326); the contingency list; the gym-lifter link (PDF 329); baseline line 185. | Optional: add `[[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]]` (6.14.4, same book) to Related. |\n| claim--hettinger-four-features-that-stimulate-strength-gains | GO | checked: all four points verbatim (PDF 325 / p.314); \"modified and extended by more recent research\". The Ch1 threshold entry does say \"one-third\" (1953) and \"80-95%\", so the comparison holds. Baseline line 108 verbatim. | none |\n| claim--heavy-medium-light-alternation-beats-monotonic-increasing-loading | GO | checked: the Fry 1994a/b sentence and its hedge \"would appear to\" (PDF 326); the Kraemer and Hakkinen citations; the PDF 329 repeat. The Fig 6.4 caption and the L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S labels match the image (PDF 333). Baseline line 106 verbatim. The Verkhoshansky \"word play\" point is correctly given \"as reported by Siff\". | none |\n| framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap | GO | checked: definition; the units; 5-10 days / 1-4 months / 10-12 months; minimum two workout types; Bulgarian >5 sessions; the meso/macro swap (PDF 327 / p.316); \"commonly a week long\" (PDF 337); baseline line 179. | Optional dedup link: `[[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]`. |\n| claim--conversion-phase-is-a-misnomer | GO | checked: the whole quote (PDF 327). The Example quote \"against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block\" is real, at PDF 304 (speed-strength and complex-technique sequence, step 3). Baseline line 180 verbatim. | none |\n| framework--matveyev-microcycle-and-mesocycle-types-and-sequences | GO | checked: the five microcycle types (PDF 327); the four sample mesocycles; the six mesocycle types; the endurance and speed-strength sequences (PDF 328); baseline lines 186 and 187. The tension between \"ordinary\" and alternation is handled correctly as Inference. | none |\n| heuristic--shock-microcycle-limits | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 327-328), including the 3-4 per year and one double shock per year; baseline line 142 verbatim. | none |\n| heuristic--gpp-and-spp-form-one-unit-curtail-gpp-for-advanced-athletes | GO | checked: Bondarchuk 1979; GPP contents; the hypertrophy-phase strength-deficit decision; the curtailment for advanced athletes; GPP inside SPP (PDF 328, where \"SPF\" is an OCR error for SPP); the transition warning (PDF 329); baseline line 105 and its \"Not claimed in the interview\" note. | none |\n| heuristic--in-season-stabilisation-twice-weekly-30-minutes | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 329 / p.318). Baseline line 105 is quoted correctly with both caveats. The Example is labelled Inference. | none |\n| heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume | GO | checked: quote verbatim (PDF 329). The Inference \"15% vs 37% in Fig 6.3A, about 41%\" matches the image (PDF 333: 15, 23, 37, 25); 15/37 = 40.5%. Baseline line 106. | none |\n| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability | FIX | The entry says \"the **third** bullet in the source list ('the premature introduction of skills')\". On PDF 329 / p.318 the bullets are, in order: (1) \"eliminating any factors from training which disrupt learning of technique\", (2) \"the premature introduction of skills ...\", (3) \"the ability to master mental control ...\". It is the second bullet. Everything else checked: 70-80%; Matveyev 1977; gym lifters; feedforward and feedback (PDF 330). | Change \"the third bullet\" to \"the second bullet\". |\n| framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model | GO | checked: the Fig 6.1 image (PDF 330). Volume falls and intensity rises through the preparation phase and TF1, and they cross inside TF1; the technique curve rises to a plateau in the competition phase. All 8 items (PDF 330-331) and Medvedev 1986 checked. The item 5 voice note is correct. The Evans link is a fair cross-reference. | none |\n| principle--sporting-form-cannot-be-held-permanently-matveyev | GO | checked: the Ryun Fig 6.2 image (PDF 332). The tallest bars, about 170 km, are opening microcycle 1 and INTERM microcycle 1; the opening block has a short second bar about 10-15 km tall; the late competitive bars are about 35, 45, 40 and 22 km; the result points run about 97-100% with WR dots. The two figures captioned \"Figure 6.2\" are confirmed (PDF 332 and 333). The question and answer quotes are verbatim (PDF 331). | none |\n| framework--six-broad-periodisation-schemes | GO | checked: all six schemes and their numbers (PDF 332-333); the \"system 4\" sentence (PDF 334), correctly flagged as probable misnumbering; the Issurin 2016 caveat is aimed at the right target. | none |\n| case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions | GO | checked against the images (PDF 333-334): Fig 6.2A 42/23/35; B 54/30/16; Fig 6.3A 15/23/37/25; B 13/21/30/36; Fig 6.4 L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S; Fig 6.5 L,M,L,S,M,L,M,L, whose caption does compare it with Fig 6.3; Fig 6.6 L,L,S,M,L,L,S,M. All cells correct. `untested` is appropriate. | none |\n| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | FIX | The flagged slip is real: on PDF 334 / p.323, 3 x 3 at 75 kg = 75/120 = 62.5%, so the Inference is correct. But the entry also says \"the worked example drops a 50% set as a warm-up, **which follows the 70% rule**.\" It does not. The example keeps the 80 kg x 8 set, which is 80/120 = **66.7% of 1RM**, below the \"less than about 70% of 1RM\" warm-up exclusion printed in the paragraph just above. Under Chernyak's >40% rule, the 60 kg (50%) set would count. The worked example follows neither stated rule. | Replace with: \"the worked example drops the 60 kg (50%) set but keeps the 80 kg set (66.7% of 1RM), so it follows neither the 70% warm-up rule nor Chernyak's 40% rule (PDF 334 / p.323). Record which rule a log uses.\" |\n| framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording | GO | checked: the five zones (PDF 335 text). Table 6.1 column I is headed \"60%-\" in the image, so the flagged inconsistency is real and correctly described. Fig 6.7: A peaks at zone III, about 40; B is skewed low; C is skewed high (by eye). The near-miss rule and the notation (superscripts are set counts, 10(60)^1, 8(70)^1, 6(80)^2) checked on PDF 336. | Optional: note that Fig 6.7's own axis labels zone I \"50+\", which agrees with the text and not with Table 6.1. |\n| claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification | GO | checked: all cited Table 6.2 cells against the image (PDF 336): 52 kg 51.0/63.5/70.0/78.0/80.0; 75 kg 70.0/92.0/99.0/111.0/116.0; 100 kg 82.0/101.0/113.0/125.0/132.0; +110 kg 86.0/109.0/120.0/130.0/147.0. The \"optimal\" versus \"upper limits\" tension is real. Dvorkin bands 50-70 / 60-90 / 80-90%. | none |\n| heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions | GO | checked: Russian versus Bulgarian 1RM, novice pre-testing, 5RM/6RM (PDF 337); Fig 6.8 sequence; the \"work backwards\" quote (PDF 337); Table 6.3 means; \"guidelines, not scientific computations\"; the modular day (PDF 338); baseline lines 183, 107, 185. | Optional dedup links: `[[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]]` (6.15), `[[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]]` (6.14.5), `[[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]`. |\n| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | FIX | All quotes and numbers check (PDF 339-340: RPE scales 1-3, 1-10 and 1-5; \"identify any decrease\"; Wiener 1948; Bulgarian -10 kg / +5 kg; minor exercises; manual labourers). The voice Inference overreaches. PDF 339-340 name neither author in the third person and do not cite Siff for the method (Siff 1990 is cited only for order and causal analysis on PDF 342). \"This textbook introduced\" (PDF 345) shows that Siff, writing 6.3, credits *the book*, not himself. The third-person test therefore does not establish Siff here. | Replace the voice sentence with: \"Voice: pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author. Section 6.3, written in Siff's voice, refers back to it as what 'this textbook introduced' (PDF 345 / p.334). Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" |\n| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | FIX | Novik protocol (PDF 340) and PWC-170 protocol and formula (PDF 341) checked; all 11 Table 6.5 rows match the image. Overreach: the entry lists \"pulls 0%\" as part of the table's inconsistency with \"+70%\". The text applies the 70% only \"in each case **where there is significant eccentric action**\" (PDF 341), so 0% for the clean and snatch pulls fits that qualifier. The real inconsistencies are the ratios above or between: bench +100%, squat clean about +88%, split clean +40%, split snatch about +33%, good morning about +49%, jerk about +11%, power clean about +6%. | Reword to: \"...the table's ratios differ: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit, but bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it, and split clean, split snatch, good morning, jerk and power clean (about +6% to +49%) fall between; the pulls at 0% fit the 'significant eccentric action' qualifier.\" |\n| framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation | GO | checked: the 5 HR modes and 80% max for rowers, BP (PDF 341); Shishkin 1989, Siff 1977, 5-6 h; the Ceff formula and Spiridonova and Kuznetsova 1986; Rodionov; the palpation sites (PDF 342). The Inference arithmetic is correct (300/180 = 1.67; 300/190 = 1.58). `untested` fits. | none |\n| framework--order-and-causal-analysis-for-judging-training-schemes | GO | checked: Siff 1990 (PDF 342); all four causal methods with their limitations; \"Medeleev 1869\" as printed (PDF 343 image). The Mill's-methods remark is labelled Inference. | none |\n| claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations | GO | checked against the image (PDF 343 / p.332): 12-15, 9-10, 6-7, 3-4, 2-3, 3-4 and 1-2 weeks. They sum to 36 (minimum) and 45 (maximum), so \"about 36-45\" is correct. Contradiction (a) is real: the opening says the most frequent weeks include \"high volume and high intensity\", while rec. 3 gives large/high only 2-3 weeks. Contradiction (b) is real: rec. 1 versus rec. 2 on medium/medium. `contested` is justified as a same-page contradiction. Chernyak is cited as \"1978\" on PDF 334 and as \"et al, 1979\" here; the entry uses the correct form for each page. | none |\n| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | The voice is correct: PDF 344-345 name Verkhoshansky in the third person throughout and say \"this textbook introduced\" and \"this book paid special attention\". Siff's framing and Verkhoshansky's points are not mixed up, and the Zheljazkov quotes are verbatim (PDF 345-346). Both sides are on the cited pages. The other library sources are named correctly (Issurin 2016, Harries, Afonso, Evans); no Bompa entry in the vault takes a side, so none is missing. **Misattribution:** \"British runners declined after adopting 'Matveyev's block scheme' widely after 1980 (citing Horwill 1992, 1995; Tschiene 1991, 1992)\". On PDF 344, Horwill is cited for condemning \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation\". Tschiene is cited separately, for the claim that \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\". The British-decline claim is Verkhoshansky's own statement. | Split the citations: \"British runners declined after adopting 'Matveyev's block scheme' widely after 1980 (Verkhoshansky's own claim); he cites Horwill (1992, 1995) condemning 'the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation', and Tschiene (1991, 1992) concluding that periodisation 'has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965' (PDF 344 / p.333).\" Optional: add Verkhoshansky's point that periodisation reflects Soviet five-year-plan thinking, and Zheljazkov's partial agreement with it (PDF 345). |\n\n## Duplicate check\n\n- Vault and staging grep for: Rule of Sixty, shock microcycle, cybernetic, Chernyak, Zheljazkov, conversion phase, PWC, coefficient of effectiveness, order analysis, stabilisation phase, Ryun, Kotov/Dyson/Pikhala, reliability of technique. No existing entry restates any in-scope entry.\n- Within the batch: the 60% rule entry and the heavy/medium/light entry both quote the PDF 329 paragraph. The angles are distinct (a number versus the alternation claim), and the two cross-link. The Vorobyev case study and the heavy/medium/light entry share Fig 6.4 as an example; that is acceptable. None needs merging.\n- Against Ch6b-d (out of scope): the backwards-plan entry overlaps in topic with 6.15's eight-step sequence and 6.14.5's calendar rules, but the angle is different (classical bar-chart planning versus Verkhoshansky's programming). Link them; do not merge. The event-specific entry overlaps with 6.14.4's technical-mastery principle; again, link only.\n- Against Bompa, Issurin and Evans: the training-cycle and backwards-plan entries sit beside Evans's `bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` and `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`. The book-specific details (meso/macro swap, 5RM estimation, Fig 6.8) are new angles; add the links.\n\n## Missing (important ideas with no entry)\n\n1. **Chronobiology and periodisation** (PDF 346-347 / p.335-336). The circadian system and the SCN entrained by the retina; exercise and emotional arousal shift rhythms through intergeniculate and raphe inputs; this is offered as possible future corroboration of periodisation. It is the section's only physiological argument for cycling.\n2. **Verkhoshansky's \"communism\" argument and Zheljazkov's partial concession** (PDF 345 / p.334). Periodisation's cyclicity was modelled on Soviet five-year plans, and its precise quantities were meant to remove subjectivity. Zheljazkov agrees that society shapes training but says the effect is not always negative. It could be a sentence in the controversy entry.\n3. **Tschiene's claim** that periodisation is unchanged since Matveyev's 1965 formalisation, and that coaching doctrine has not advanced (PDF 344). It is only lost through the citation slip above.\n4. **\"The organisation of training is as much a matter of art, trial-and-error, and intuition\"** and the fractal/chaotic-systems rationale (PDF 344). It is quoted inside the controversy entry; a standalone principle could reconcile it with 6.17's \"programming is an art\" entry. Low priority.\n5. **Matveyev's critique of the \"notorious peaks\" versus season-long demand** is covered. Nothing else of weight is uncaptured; 6.1-6.3 is otherwise densely covered.\n\n## Reciprocal link suggestions\n\n- `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice` <-> `heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation`\n- `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training` and `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` <-> `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions`\n- `elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` <-> `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`\n- `principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm` <-> `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov`\n\n## Counts\n\nGO 22, FIX 6, NO-GO 0.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. early-history: \"on the next page\" becomes \"on the same page\" (PDF 325).\n2. progress-skills: \"third bullet\" becomes \"second bullet\" (PDF 329).\n3. volume-intensity calculation: the worked example keeps an 80 kg set (66.7% of 1RM), so it does not follow the 70% rule; reword (PDF 334).\n4. cybernetic periodisation: soften the Siff attribution; the page names neither author (PDF 339-340, 345).\n5. heart-rate tests: reword the Table 6.5 inconsistency; the pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier (PDF 341).\n6. periodisation controversy: separate the Horwill and Tschiene citations from the British-decline claim and add Tschiene's actual point (PDF 344).\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.1-6.3 (Ch6a), 28 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 6 FIX rows, optional Related links on 4 GO rows, 5 missing ideas).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 324-347 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6a_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 325, 329, 334, 339, 340, 341, 344, 345, 346 and 347 to `staging/_renders/f6a_p<N>.png` at 200 dpi. I opened PDF 325 (Dyson's phases and the \"may create the impression\" warning under \"Ways of Organising Training\"), 329 (the three bullets after \"zone-by-zone mastering\"), 334 (Exercise A worked example, the 40% and 70% rules), 340 (cybernetic periodisation, no author named), 341 (Table 6.5 and the \"significant eccentric action\" sentence), 344 (Horwill, British decline, Tschiene), 345 (\"this textbook introduced\", the five-year-plan passage, Zheljazkov's partial agreement) and 346 (chronobiology). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 28 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt`. I edited the 6 flagged entries, plus 3 GO entries for the optional Related links. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (28 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6b, 6c or 6d file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md | **Fix 1.** \"warn on the next page\" is now \"warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\". Verified on PDF 325 / p.314: the Dyson list, the \"Ways of Organising Training\" heading and the \"may create the impression ... far from the truth\" sentence are all on that page. |\n| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md | **Fix 2.** \"the third bullet\" is now \"the second bullet in the source list (\"the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification\", PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318)\". Verified on the image: the bullets are (1) eliminating disruptive factors, (2) premature introduction of skills, (3) mental control. |\n| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md | **Fix 3.** \"drops a 50% set as a warm-up, which follows the 70% rule\" is replaced: the example drops the 60 kg (50%) set but keeps the 80 kg set (66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM). \"Inference from the printed numbers: the example therefore follows neither the 70% warm-up rule (which would also drop the 80 kg set) nor Chernyak's 40% rule (which would keep the 60 kg set)\", with the PDF 334 / p.323 locator. \"Record which rule a log uses\" is kept. The arithmetic is labelled Inference because it is the fixer's, not the authors'. |\n| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md | **Fix 4.** The \"Voice, Inference: ... read as Siff's own contribution\" sentence is replaced. Now: pages 339-340 name neither author and address the reader as \"you\"; Section 6.3, in Siff's voice, refers back to \"methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" that \"this textbook introduced\" and to \"the section on Cybernetic Periodisation\" (PDF 345 / p.334, verified on the image); \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" |\n| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md | **Fix 5.** The Table 6.5 sentence now gives all eleven ratios, recomputed from the image of PDF 341 / p.330: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit the 70%; bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it; split clean about +40%, split snatch about +33%, deep good morning about +49%, jerk about +11% and power clean about +6% fall between; the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier. \"pulls 0%\" is no longer listed as an inconsistency. |\n| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md | **Fix 6.** The British-decline claim is now \"(Verkhoshansky's own claim)\". Horwill (1992, 1995) is cited for \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation as used by some runners in different countries\". Tschiene (1991, 1992) is cited for \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\" and \"many coach's doctrines have not advanced\" (PDF 344 / p.333, verified on the image). The \"word play\" sentence now carries its own locator, PDF 345 / p.334. **Missing idea 2** added to the Verkhoshansky paragraph (the communism / \"five year plans\" / \"subjectivity and emotiveness\" passage) and to the Zheljazkov paragraph (his partial agreement, quoted), both PDF 345 / p.334. **Missing idea 3** (Tschiene) is covered by the fix. **Missing idea 1** added as a paragraph, \"Chronobiology, the section's closing argument\" (PDF 346-347 / p.335-336), with \"Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles\" and a labelled Inference. The What it is range is now PDF 344-347 / printed 333-336. **Accuracy fix beyond the review:** the Example said British \"middle-distance runners won Olympic gold before, not after\" adopting the scheme. PDF 344 does not say that: \"middle distances\" refers to Soviet runners, and the British claim is \"did not gain gold medals on Olympic games over the last 30 years, but produced great achievements before they used such concepts\". The Example now quotes that sentence. **Optional link** added: [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]]. |\n| heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation.md | Optional link: [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]] added to Related. |\n| framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap.md | Optional link: [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]] added to Related. |\n| heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md | Optional links: [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]] added to Related. |\n\nAll new link targets exist today: the four Supertraining targets in the staging book folder, and the two Evans targets in both the staging library and the vault.\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Chronobiology and periodisation (PDF 346-347) | Folded into the controversy entry as a paragraph. It is the closing subsection of 6.3 and offers itself as \"future corroboration\" in the same debate. The paragraph says `untested`, and why. The entry-level `contested` stays, because it describes the Verkhoshansky-Zheljazkov dispute. No new entry: the passage is a pointer to a research field with no training data, not a substantive idea that needs its own entry. |\n| 2 | Five-year-plan argument and Zheljazkov's partial agreement (PDF 345) | Folded into the controversy entry's Verkhoshansky and Zheljazkov paragraphs, quoted. |\n| 3 | Tschiene's claim (PDF 344) | Covered by fix 6. |\n| 4 | \"art, trial-and-error, and intuition\" and the fractal rationale (PDF 344) | Already quoted in the controversy entry's Siff's-framing paragraph. The review calls it low priority. I added the reciprocal link to the 6.17 \"programming is an art\" principle instead of a new entry. |\n| 5 | Matveyev's \"notorious peaks\" critique | The review says it is already covered; confirmed in the Verkhoshansky paragraph. |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6a.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\nNone of the six fixes was wrong. Every claim held on the page image. Notes on how they were applied:\n1. **Fix 3 and fix 5 are labelled Inference.** The review's replacement text reads as plain statements, but the percentages are the fixer's arithmetic, not printed values, so the entry labels them \"Inference from the printed numbers\" / \"Inference from the printed values\".\n2. **Fix 4 quote widened.** The review quotes only \"this textbook introduced\". The entry now also quotes 6.3's explicit pointer \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" on the same page, which is the stronger evidence that 6.3 refers back to this method.\n3. **Not applied: the optional Fig 6.7 axis note** on `framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording.md` (zone I labelled \"50+\"). The task limited optional work to Related links and missing ideas. It remains open for the coordinator.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (6b-6d files, not edited by me) the review suggests: `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice` should link back to [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]]. `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training` and `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` should link back to [[heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions]]. `principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm` should link back to [[claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov]]. The Evans entries are outside this book.\n- Confidence tallies are unchanged.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder:\n\n```\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:(Verkhoshansky's own claim)\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:He cites Horwill (1992, 1995)\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:20:five year plans\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:22:partly agrees\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:24:Chronobiology, the section's closing argument\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:24:Confidence for this part: `untested`\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:32:did not gain gold medals on Olympic games\nclaim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md:34:programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm\nframework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md:24:pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author\nframework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md:24:treat it as the authors'\nframework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md:30:warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\nframework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap.md:34:bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types\nframework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md:30:keeps the 80 kg set, which is 66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM\nframework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md:30:follows neither the 70% warm-up rule\nheuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation.md:26:technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice\nheuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training\nheuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates\nheuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions.md:32:ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan\nheuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md:24:bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it\nheuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md:24:the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier\nheuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md:22:the second bullet in the source list\n```\n\nOld phrases (\"on the next page\", \"the third bullet\", \"which follows the 70% rule\", \"read as Siff's own contribution\", \"pulls 0%)\", \"(citing Horwill\", \"won Olympic gold before\"): scoped files still holding any of them: none.\n\nMechanical greps over all 28 scoped files:\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n```\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nNo 6a file has a lint line. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.\n","numLines":171,"startLine":1,"totalLines":171}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8d47c593-55bf-4957-8d98-fc8eda9774e5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
