524-caa52ca3b9d0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:15.348Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b1f1a78a-bb74-4b3d-b524-caa52ca3b9d0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMPW6gHBZwZGvrJfa2PN19","type":"tool_result","content":"=== claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.3\"\n     7\ttopic: the periodisation controversy\n     8\tcategory: [periodization, matveyev, critique, elite-athletes, evidence-quality]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Periodisation Controversy: Verkhoshansky's Critique of Classical Periodisation and Zheljazkov's Reply\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 6.3 (PDF p. 344-347 / printed p. 333-336; verified against the page images) is Siff's voice by the book's own signals: it names Verkhoshansky in the third person throughout and refers to what \"this textbook introduced.\" It has three voices.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Siff's framing.** Classical Matveyev periodisation is widely used in the West, yet \"even among Russian researchers and scientists, this approach to training has attracted some fierce criticism.\" Siff accepts part of it: the belief that periodisation is \"an exact science\" with computerised prescriptions is wrong, because \"The body is not an exactly controlled, deterministic system\"; training is \"as much a matter of art, trial-and-error, and intuition, as it is of science,\" so schemes \"should serve as approximate guidelines\" modified by physiological and psychological markers (PDF p. 344 / printed p. 333). He concludes \"organising training according to the classical model of Matveyev can be used only by athletes of low qualification for certain phases of the programme,\" the reason the book introduced ongoing subjective and objective assessment (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). He also objects that classical computation \"utilises only two factors,\" volume and intensity.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Verkhoshansky (1997), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"is not a suitable model for training for elite sportsmen\" and should be \"rejected or modified\" for modern crowded calendars; \"at best, separate forms of periodisation should be used by novice and young athletes.\" The mechanical division into periods and mesocycles rests on 1950s Soviet experience \"mainly on the example of three sports, namely swimming, weightlifting, track and field athletics.\" Training should rest \"much more on physiology.\" Plans should meet demands \"distributed throughout a competitive season\" rather than \"notorious peaks of sports form.\" Kenyan runners \"have never implemented periodisation\"; British runners declined after adopting \"Matveyev's block scheme\" widely after 1980 (Verkhoshansky's own claim). He cites Horwill (1992, 1995) condemning \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation as used by some runners in different countries\", and Tschiene (1991, 1992) concluding that \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\" and that \"many coach's doctrines have not advanced\" (PDF p. 344 / printed p. 333). He rejects \"laws\" of training and dismisses \"'undulating models and 'wave models'\" as \"word play\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). He also observes that \"the general paradigm of periodisation was based heavily upon the philosophies and methodologies of communism\": its cyclical nature \"was strongly influenced by the 'five year plans' and other cycles of productivity in the soviet system,\" and \"the precise calculation of training quantities reflected attempts to minimise the unscientific factors of subjectivity and emotiveness\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334).\n    21\t\n    22\t**Zheljazkov (1998), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"still managed to be utilised very effectively for many years in training numerous elite soviet and Eastern European athletes\"; it is \"erroneous\" to treat Matveyev's concept as \"the complete theory of sports training\"; Verkhoshansky's charges of \"primitive methodology\" and \"scholastic demagogy\" are \"a unprofessional personal attack\"; national rises and falls in running are natural, not caused by periodisation. On the communism point he partly agrees: \"models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact on the nature of sports training, but ... this happens in all countries and should not necessarily be taken to mean that this interaction between sport and society is always negative\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). Classical periodisation \"has not lost its fundamental importance\" but has \"serious qualitative limitations\" to be modified by research on \"exhaustion, adaptation and restoration,\" since regeneration is \"nonlinear and heterochronical\"; applied sport science \"does not at all replace the entire concept of periodisation, but enriches it\" (PDF p. 345-346 / printed p. 334-335).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Chronobiology, the section's closing argument.** The subsection \"Chronobiology and Periodisation\" (PDF p. 346-347 / printed p. 335-336, verified against the page image of PDF 346) offers biological clocks as possible support: \"Future corroboration of the principles of periodisation may rely on some of the advances being made in the field of chronobiology.\" The text describes a master circadian pacemaker, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, \"entrained to the cycle of day and night by nervous input from the retina,\" which \"can also be shifted or entrained by other stimuli, such as an appropriately timed bouts of exercise\"; two further inputs, \"one from the thalamic intergeniculate structure, and another from the midbrain raphe nuclei,\" \"appear to play an important role in entraining or shifting rhythms by means of exercise and emotional arousal.\" It concludes only that \"The possible link to periodisation of physical training then becomes apparent\" (PDF p. 346 / printed p. 335). This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training. Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles; Inference: a 24-hour clock says nothing direct about mesocycle or macrocycle length.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — Each side argues from different evidence: Verkhoshansky from the 1950s origins of the model, its three-sport base and national running records; Zheljazkov from decades of Soviet and Eastern European elite success. Neither gives controlled data on these pages, and both national-results arguments are correlational (Inference).\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — When a plan treats the annual chart as fixed. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)), and \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: both sides would accept the roadmap framing, and Verkhoshansky's season-long demands point fits weekly football games better than a single peak.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested.** Same question (is classical periodisation suitable for elite athletes), two named authorities, opposite answers, on PDF p. 344 versus p. 345. Note the internal tension with the book's own method: Verkhoshansky dismisses \"undulating models\" as word play (PDF p. 345), while the authors endorse heavy, medium and light alternation (PDF p. 326, 329); Inference: his complaint is about relabelling, not about load variation working, so it is a tension in emphasis, not a contradiction. This also touches Eric's baseline directly: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Library sources: Issurin 2016 judged evidence for Verkhoshansky's own concentrated-unidirectional alternative insufficient for team sports ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]); Harries et al. 2015 found linear and undulating programmes give similar strength gains, and Afonso et al. 2019 argue that periodised-versus-non-periodised meta-analyses did not isolate periodisation's effect (vault research-papers entries). Inference: the modern papers support neither side's strong claim. Evans frames the same split as \"classic\" versus \"non-classic\" literature ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--periodization-evidence-classic-vs-nonclassic-literature-debate]]).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The authors' own: Verkhoshansky's claim that British runners \"who used the Russian concept of periodisation did not gain gold medals on Olympic games over the last 30 years, but produced great achievements before they used such concepts,\" having adopted Matveyev's scheme widely after 1980, against Zheljazkov's reply that \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" (PDF p. 344-345 / printed p. 333-334).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model]], [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[framework--six-broad-periodisation-schemes]], [[claim--heavy-medium-light-alternation-beats-monotonic-increasing-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[research-papers/harries-2015-systematic-review-and-meta-analysis-of-linear-and-undulating-periodized-resistance-trainin]], [[research-papers/afonso-2019-a-systematic-review-of-meta-analyses-comparing-periodized-and-non-periodized-exercise-prog]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--periodization-evidence-classic-vs-nonclassic-literature-debate]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]\n=== heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.2.2\"\n     7\ttopic: heart-rate work capacity tests\n     8\tcategory: [monitoring, heart-rate, work-capacity, weightlifting, testing]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Heart-Rate Work-Capacity Tests for Lifters: the Novik Squat Test and an Adapted PWC-170\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Heart rate can show what load computations miss: \"If the heart rate increases noticeably prior to the heavier or heaviest training loads in any session, it indicates the presence of significant levels of emotional arousal and a more demanding level of training intensity, even though the periodisation computations might indicate otherwise\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, verified against the page image). Measure HR before, immediately after, and each minute for 4 minutes after an exercise; for resistance training, take recovery HR after \"repetition squats, cleans and bench press\" rather than on a treadmill, \"because of the effects such as local muscle ischaemia.\"\n    17\t- **Novik squat test** (Novik et al, 1980, Olympic weightlifters): at the start of a cycle, find the 1RM squat; then do \"one set of 3 repetitions of squats\" at \"85-90% of this 1RM,\" and measure HR \"for a period lasting one minute.\" Repeat through the cycle: \"If the immediate post-squatting and the post-one minute heart rates decrease, then work capacity has increased and loading may be increased\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n    18\t- **Adapted PWC-170** (Karpman et al, 1982; Dvorkin, 1988), PDF p. 341 / printed p. 330, verified against the page image: with the 10RM load, rest about 5 minutes, do 5 reps, measure HR (F1); rest another 5 minutes, do 8-10 reps, measure HR (F2). PWC-170 = N1 + (N2 - N1)(170 - F1)/(F2 - F1), where N = 9.8 x W x R x D, and D is the bar distance as a fraction of lifter height H (Table 6.5). The squat \"is far more commonly used.\" Table 6.5 values (concentric D / equivalent D for total work): full back squat 0.35 H / 0.60 H; half squat 0.22 / 0.37; bench press 0.28 / 0.56; squat clean 0.67 / 1.26; split clean 0.55 / 0.77; clean pull 0.50 / 0.50; snatch pull 0.59 / 0.59; power clean 0.65 / 0.69; deep good morning 0.35 / 0.52; jerk from chest 0.54 / 0.60; split snatch 0.67 / 0.89.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: a lower HR response to the same submaximal squat effort means higher work capacity (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329). No physiological explanation beyond that is given.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — As a low-cost in-house readiness and capacity check during a strength block, using an exercise athletes already do. Inference: no line in `docs/method-baseline.md` describes a lift-specific heart-rate response, so the Novik test would add to Baylor's force-plate and GPS monitoring rather than replace any of it.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The Novik test requires a measured 1RM squat, which the baseline says is not routinely tested (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material), citing Q10). Table 6.5 is not internally consistent with its own note: the text says eccentric action was \"estimated to add 70% more work in each case where there is significant eccentric action,\" but the table's ratios differ (Inference from the printed values, PDF p. 341 / printed p. 330, verified against the page image: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit the 70%; bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it; split clean about +40%, split snatch about +33%, deep good morning about +49%, jerk about +11% and power clean about +6% fall between; the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier). The book does not explain the variation. HR is also raised by \"emotional arousal,\" so a high reading does not by itself separate fatigue from nerves.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: a weightlifter squats 3 reps at 85-90% of 1RM at intervals through a cycle; falling immediate and 1-minute post-set HR signals higher work capacity and permits more load (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[research-papers/thorpe-2017-monitoring-fatigue-status-in-elite-team-sport-athletes-implications-for-practice]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bddb6677-de1b-4e31-b2ae-80b0a91b48d2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:15.613Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.3\"\n     7\ttopic: the periodisation controversy\n     8\tcategory: [periodization, matveyev, critique, elite-athletes, evidence-quality]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Periodisation Controversy: Verkhoshansky's Critique of Classical Periodisation and Zheljazkov's Reply\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 6.3 (PDF p. 344-347 / printed p. 333-336; verified against the page images) is Siff's voice by the book's own signals: it names Verkhoshansky in the third person throughout and refers to what \"this textbook introduced.\" It has three voices.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Siff's framing.** Classical Matveyev periodisation is widely used in the West, yet \"even among Russian researchers and scientists, this approach to training has attracted some fierce criticism.\" Siff accepts part of it: the belief that periodisation is \"an exact science\" with computerised prescriptions is wrong, because \"The body is not an exactly controlled, deterministic system\"; training is \"as much a matter of art, trial-and-error, and intuition, as it is of science,\" so schemes \"should serve as approximate guidelines\" modified by physiological and psychological markers (PDF p. 344 / printed p. 333). He concludes \"organising training according to the classical model of Matveyev can be used only by athletes of low qualification for certain phases of the programme,\" the reason the book introduced ongoing subjective and objective assessment (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). He also objects that classical computation \"utilises only two factors,\" volume and intensity.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Verkhoshansky (1997), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"is not a suitable model for training for elite sportsmen\" and should be \"rejected or modified\" for modern crowded calendars; \"at best, separate forms of periodisation should be used by novice and young athletes.\" The mechanical division into periods and mesocycles rests on 1950s Soviet experience \"mainly on the example of three sports, namely swimming, weightlifting, track and field athletics.\" Training should rest \"much more on physiology.\" Plans should meet demands \"distributed throughout a competitive season\" rather than \"notorious peaks of sports form.\" Kenyan runners \"have never implemented periodisation\"; British runners declined after adopting \"Matveyev's block scheme\" widely after 1980 (Verkhoshansky's own claim). He cites Horwill (1992, 1995) condemning \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation as used by some runners in different countries\", and Tschiene (1991, 1992) concluding that \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\" and that \"many coach's doctrines have not advanced\" (PDF p. 344 / printed p. 333). He rejects \"laws\" of training and dismisses \"'undulating models and 'wave models'\" as \"word play\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). He also observes that \"the general paradigm of periodisation was based heavily upon the philosophies and methodologies of communism\": its cyclical nature \"was strongly influenced by the 'five year plans' and other cycles of productivity in the soviet system,\" and \"the precise calculation of training quantities reflected attempts to minimise the unscientific factors of subjectivity and emotiveness\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334).\n    21\t\n    22\t**Zheljazkov (1998), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"still managed to be utilised very effectively for many years in training numerous elite soviet and Eastern European athletes\"; it is \"erroneous\" to treat Matveyev's concept as \"the complete theory of sports training\"; Verkhoshansky's charges of \"primitive methodology\" and \"scholastic demagogy\" are \"a unprofessional personal attack\"; national rises and falls in running are natural, not caused by periodisation. On the communism point he partly agrees: \"models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact on the nature of sports training, but ... this happens in all countries and should not necessarily be taken to mean that this interaction between sport and society is always negative\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). Classical periodisation \"has not lost its fundamental importance\" but has \"serious qualitative limitations\" to be modified by research on \"exhaustion, adaptation and restoration,\" since regeneration is \"nonlinear and heterochronical\"; applied sport science \"does not at all replace the entire concept of periodisation, but enriches it\" (PDF p. 345-346 / printed p. 334-335).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Chronobiology, the section's closing argument.** The subsection \"Chronobiology and Periodisation\" (PDF p. 346-347 / printed p. 335-336, verified against the page image of PDF 346) offers biological clocks as possible support: \"Future corroboration of the principles of periodisation may rely on some of the advances being made in the field of chronobiology.\" The text describes a master circadian pacemaker, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, \"entrained to the cycle of day and night by nervous input from the retina,\" which \"can also be shifted or entrained by other stimuli, such as an appropriately timed bouts of exercise\"; two further inputs, \"one from the thalamic intergeniculate structure, and another from the midbrain raphe nuclei,\" \"appear to play an important role in entraining or shifting rhythms by means of exercise and emotional arousal.\" It concludes only that \"The possible link to periodisation of physical training then becomes apparent\" (PDF p. 346 / printed p. 335). This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training. Confidence for this part: `untested`. The text gives no study linking circadian mechanisms to multi-week or annual training cycles; Inference: a 24-hour clock says nothing direct about mesocycle or macrocycle length.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — Each side argues from different evidence: Verkhoshansky from the 1950s origins of the model, its three-sport base and national running records; Zheljazkov from decades of Soviet and Eastern European elite success. Neither gives controlled data on these pages, and both national-results arguments are correlational (Inference).\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — When a plan treats the annual chart as fixed. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)), and \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: both sides would accept the roadmap framing, and Verkhoshansky's season-long demands point fits weekly football games better than a single peak.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested.** Same question (is classical periodisation suitable for elite athletes), two named authorities, opposite answers, on PDF p. 344 versus p. 345. Note the internal tension with the book's own method: Verkhoshansky dismisses \"undulating models\" as word play (PDF p. 345), while the authors endorse heavy, medium and light alternation (PDF p. 326, 329); Inference: his complaint is about relabelling, not about load variation working, so it is a tension in emphasis, not a contradiction. This also touches Eric's baseline directly: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Library sources: Issurin 2016 judged evidence for Verkhoshansky's own concentrated-unidirectional alternative insufficient for team sports ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]); Harries et al. 2015 found linear and undulating programmes give similar strength gains, and Afonso et al. 2019 argue that periodised-versus-non-periodised meta-analyses did not isolate periodisation's effect (vault research-papers entries). Inference: the modern papers support neither side's strong claim. Evans frames the same split as \"classic\" versus \"non-classic\" literature ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--periodization-evidence-classic-vs-nonclassic-literature-debate]]).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The authors' own: Verkhoshansky's claim that British runners \"who used the Russian concept of periodisation did not gain gold medals on Olympic games over the last 30 years, but produced great achievements before they used such concepts,\" having adopted Matveyev's scheme widely after 1980, against Zheljazkov's reply that \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" (PDF p. 344-345 / printed p. 333-334).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model]], [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[framework--six-broad-periodisation-schemes]], [[claim--heavy-medium-light-alternation-beats-monotonic-increasing-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[research-papers/harries-2015-systematic-review-and-meta-analysis-of-linear-and-undulating-periodized-resistance-trainin]], [[research-papers/afonso-2019-a-systematic-review-of-meta-analyses-comparing-periodized-and-non-periodized-exercise-prog]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--periodization-evidence-classic-vs-nonclassic-literature-debate]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]\n=== heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.2.2\"\n     7\ttopic: heart-rate work capacity tests\n     8\tcategory: [monitoring, heart-rate, work-capacity, weightlifting, testing]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Heart-Rate Work-Capacity Tests for Lifters: the Novik Squat Test and an Adapted PWC-170\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Heart rate can show what load computations miss: \"If the heart rate increases noticeably prior to the heavier or heaviest training loads in any session, it indicates the presence of significant levels of emotional arousal and a more demanding level of training intensity, even though the periodisation computations might indicate otherwise\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, verified against the page image). Measure HR before, immediately after, and each minute for 4 minutes after an exercise; for resistance training, take recovery HR after \"repetition squats, cleans and bench press\" rather than on a treadmill, \"because of the effects such as local muscle ischaemia.\"\n    17\t- **Novik squat test** (Novik et al, 1980, Olympic weightlifters): at the start of a cycle, find the 1RM squat; then do \"one set of 3 repetitions of squats\" at \"85-90% of this 1RM,\" and measure HR \"for a period lasting one minute.\" Repeat through the cycle: \"If the immediate post-squatting and the post-one minute heart rates decrease, then work capacity has increased and loading may be increased\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n    18\t- **Adapted PWC-170** (Karpman et al, 1982; Dvorkin, 1988), PDF p. 341 / printed p. 330, verified against the page image: with the 10RM load, rest about 5 minutes, do 5 reps, measure HR (F1); rest another 5 minutes, do 8-10 reps, measure HR (F2). PWC-170 = N1 + (N2 - N1)(170 - F1)/(F2 - F1), where N = 9.8 x W x R x D, and D is the bar distance as a fraction of lifter height H (Table 6.5). The squat \"is far more commonly used.\" Table 6.5 values (concentric D / equivalent D for total work): full back squat 0.35 H / 0.60 H; half squat 0.22 / 0.37; bench press 0.28 / 0.56; squat clean 0.67 / 1.26; split clean 0.55 / 0.77; clean pull 0.50 / 0.50; snatch pull 0.59 / 0.59; power clean 0.65 / 0.69; deep good morning 0.35 / 0.52; jerk from chest 0.54 / 0.60; split snatch 0.67 / 0.89.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: a lower HR response to the same submaximal squat effort means higher work capacity (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329). No physiological explanation beyond that is given.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — As a low-cost in-house readiness and capacity check during a strength block, using an exercise athletes already do. Inference: no line in `docs/method-baseline.md` describes a lift-specific heart-rate response, so the Novik test would add to Baylor's force-plate and GPS monitoring rather than replace any of it.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The Novik test requires a measured 1RM squat, which the baseline says is not routinely tested (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material), citing Q10). Table 6.5 is not internally consistent with its own note: the text says eccentric action was \"estimated to add 70% more work in each case where there is significant eccentric action,\" but the table's ratios differ (Inference from the printed values, PDF p. 341 / printed p. 330, verified against the page image: back squat about +71% and half squat about +68% fit the 70%; bench press +100% and squat clean about +88% exceed it; split clean about +40%, split snatch about +33%, deep good morning about +49%, jerk about +11% and power clean about +6% fall between; the clean and snatch pulls at 0% fit the \"significant eccentric action\" qualifier). The book does not explain the variation. HR is also raised by \"emotional arousal,\" so a high reading does not by itself separate fatigue from nerves.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: a weightlifter squats 3 reps at 85-90% of 1RM at intervals through a cycle; falling immediate and 1-minute post-set HR signals higher work capacity and permits more load (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[research-papers/thorpe-2017-monitoring-fatigue-status-in-elite-team-sport-athletes-implications-for-practice]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b1f1a78a-bb74-4b3d-b524-caa52ca3b9d0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
