{"parentUuid":"ad7f5cad-07f5-43d0-b52e-151851213175","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BjRkYH1qcJRGf8NSV3GbGS","type":"tool_result","content":"SUPERTRAINING\nfort those who may be interested in investigating this field more thoroughly (e.g., Klein ct al, 1991; Moore-Edeet \nal, 1992; Nagai, 1992; Chadwick & Ackrill, 1996; Redfem & Lcmmer, 1997).\n6.4 Training as a Problem in M anagement\nProgramming and organisation of training require in-depth and comprehensive knowledge of the principles of \ntraining. It is appropriate, therefore, to examine programming as a problem in management.\nThe essence of management may be expressed in terms of the changcs of state of the relevant systems \nproduced by given tasks, while the criterion of effectiveness lies in its functioning or development. Consequently, \nfor practical implementation of management, it is necessary firstly to establish concepts about the structure of the \nmanaged system and the characteristics of raising it from one state to another.\nLet us examine a logical scheme of traini","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1a2fef49-0a3f-4e9f-947b-9ef2197cb07f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:11.966Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"SUPERTRAINING\nfort those who may be interested in investigating this field more thoroughly (e.g., Klein ct al, 1991; Moore-Edeet \nal, 1992; Nagai, 1992; Chadwick & Ackrill, 1996; Redfem & Lcmmer, 1997).\n6.4 Training as a Problem in M anagement\nProgramming and organisation of training require in-depth and comprehensive knowledge of the principles of \ntraining. It is appropriate, therefore, to examine programming as a problem in management.\nThe essence of management may be expressed in terms of the changcs of state of the relevant systems \nproduced by given tasks, while the criterion of effectiveness lies in its functioning or development. Consequently, \nfor practical implementation of management, it is necessary firstly to establish concepts about the structure of the \nmanaged system and the characteristics of raising it from one state to another.\nLet us examine a logical scheme of traini","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad7f5cad-07f5-43d0-b52e-151851213175","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
