{"parentUuid":"316d15c5-2732-4c2d-910a-92f93ea9ddc4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo14C7vLa3wqrgHnTUU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Controversy entry quotes and voices hold. Now the other four FIX entries."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2533,"cache_read_input_tokens":95715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2533,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffo12HmH2eZoKArM5WHF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3c72d3f3-2582-45ad-9a93-7567440565fa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:18.540Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3c72d3f3-2582-45ad-9a93-7567440565fa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo14C7vLa3wqrgHnTUU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015JZhHu9ZJZxs9iEmLDjgir","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' $f; done","description":"Read four remaining FIX entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2533,"cache_read_input_tokens":95715,"output_tokens":626,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":338},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2533},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":626,"cache_read_input_tokens":95715,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2533,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015JZhHu9ZJZxs9iEmLDjgir":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '13,$p' $f; done","description":"Read four remaining FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffo12HmH2eZoKArM5WHF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"342856e3-e8b7-4cbf-916b-ef51edd5f875","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:20.333Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"342856e3-e8b7-4cbf-916b-ef51edd5f875","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015JZhHu9ZJZxs9iEmLDjgir","type":"tool_result","content":"=== framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md\n\n# Early History of Periodisation: From Kotov's Three Stages to Dyson's Five Phases\n\n**What it is** — The authors trace training organisation from ancient practice to the first formal periodisation schemes (PDF p. 324-325 / printed p. 313-314, verified against the page images):\n- Ancient Greeks prepared for the Olympic Games with \"a preparatory training period of at least 10 months a year\" (PDF p. 324 / printed p. 313).\n- Kotov (*Olympic Sport*, 1917) divided training into general, preparatory and specific stages: the general stage developed the \"vegetative (cardiovascular-respiratory) and muscular systems\"; preparatory training of \"about two months' duration\" improved strength and muscle endurance; the specific stage had initial and main parts \"both of about four months' duration,\" but \"there was still no overall methodology of periodisation in the form of definite cycles\" (PDF p. 324 / printed p. 313).\n- Gorinewsky (1922) and Birsin (1925) took \"preliminary steps\"; sport-specific applications followed in 1938-1940 (track and field, skiing, gymnastics, boxing, water sports).\n- Pikhala (*Athletism*, Finland, 1930) used \"an undulating rhythm of training which alternates periods of work and rest\" to prevent overtraining, but planned around \"climatic seasons, rather than quantitatively determined phases of loading.\"\n- Grantyn (1939) split the year into preparatory, main and transitional periods, the transition being \"a gradual detraining to rest, followed by active rest\" with other sports such as gymnastics and jogging, which the authors call an early form of \"combinational training\" as restoration.\n- Ozolin (1949) stressed the competition calendar and climate, and active rest within the transition phase.\n- Letunov (1950) \"strongly criticised the formulation of training regimes on the basis of the competition calendar alone,\" holding that stages follow biological processes, though \"he failed to appreciate that these processes, in turn, were modified by the training loads\" (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n- Dyson (*A New System of Training*, England, 1946) gave a five-phase athletics year: non-competitive preparation (5 months), pre-competitive (1 month), initial competitive (6 weeks), main competitive (6 weeks), post-competitive (6 weeks) (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Why it works** — This is a historical account, not a mechanism. The authors' through-line is that organisation moved from seasons and calendars toward phases set by biological adaptation, and they fault Letunov for missing that the loads themselves change those biological processes (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**When to apply** — When explaining to staff or students where macro, meso and microcycle language came from, or when an older Russian or British source uses phase names that do not match current terms. Eric's baseline: \"Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 179, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence that any one historical scheme works: the authors give no outcome data for Kotov, Grantyn or Dyson. Pikhala's \"undulating rhythm\" (1930) is about alternating work and rest, and is not the same thing as the modern undulating (daily or weekly varied) resistance model; Inference: do not cite it as an origin of that model without checking Pikhala. The authors themselves warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases, that this history \"may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation,\" which they call \"far from the truth\" (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Dyson's 1946 athletics year, 5 months of non-competitive preparation with \"gym training and cross-country running,\" then 1 month of pre-competitive specific preparation, then three 6-week blocks (initial competitive, main competitive, post-competitive) (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--seasonal-trainability-hettinger-muller-ultraviolet]], [[framework--sport-alone-vs-sport-plus-supplementary-training-and-adjunct-modes]], [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--evans-history-lineage-of-sport-science-giants]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n=== heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md\n\n# Track Reliability of Technique, and Progress Only When It Has Stabilised at 70-80% or Better\n\n**What it is** — \"Even the most meticulous computation and prescription of load intensity, volume and sequencing ... is of little consequence to performance if changes in technical mastery are not constantly monitored.\" Lab tests \"simply identify the potential offered by the athlete's work capacity and not functional preparedness\" (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318, verified against the page image). The tool is *reliability of technique*: the percentage of successes in executing the same specific skills, kept separately for training and competition. \"A low competitive reliability relative to training reliability indicates that the athlete probably has difficulty in performing under conditions of high emotional excitement and needs to simulate those conditions during training\"; \"gym lifters\" lift much more in training than on the platform, where emotional stress affects \"not ... the obviously adequate levels of strength or power, but ... the technical skills required.\" Rule: \"Progression to more difficult skills should not occur until the reliability at the preceding level of complexity or intensity has stabilised at a level of at least 70-80%,\" the \"*zone-by-zone mastering of intensity* (Matveyev, 1977).\" Technical-training dose is steered by a *feedforward method* (\"the quantitatively planned long-term scheme\") and a *feedback method* (\"the ad hoc daily approach\") because technical emphasis \"can fluctuate rapidly\" (PDF p. 330 / printed p. 319, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: performance depends on \"functional preparedness, which relies on the mastery of specific motor skills,\" and \"loss of form is prematurely attributed to overtraining or deficient training\" when the real problem is inadequate monitoring and cyclical variation of skills training (PDF p. 329-330 / printed p. 318-319).\n\n**When to apply** — Any skill progression with a clear success criterion (a lift made, a drill rep executed to standard). Inference: `docs/method-baseline.md` records no explicit technique-reliability metric, so this is a gap the passage could fill. The authors later add a coach-scored Rating of Technique column to the session log (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328; see [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 70-80% threshold has no cited data beyond the Matveyev label, and it needs \"zones of intensity or complexity\" to be \"formally laid down for each aspect of a given sport\" first. Inference: in open, reactive team-sport skills a success rate is harder to define than in a weightlifting attempt, so the threshold transfers best to closed skills. Inference: the second bullet in the source list (\"the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification\", PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318) reads as a factor to avoid, not one to stress; the source wording is ambiguous.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the \"gym lifter\" whose training lifts far exceed platform lifts, read as a technical and emotional problem rather than a strength deficit (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]], [[framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model]], [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]\n=== framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md\n\n# Calculating Volume, Mean Intensity and Relative Intensity, and Why Averages Mislead\n\n**What it is** — Volume is \"intended to provide some measure of the total work done over a given period and intensity is a measure of the average force exerted\"; approximate measures replace \"the precise methods of physics\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323, verified against the page image).\n- **Volume** of an exercise (kg) = weight x repetitions; workout volume = sum over \"all the major exercises.\"\n- **Number of lifts** = total repetitions.\n- **Intensity** of an exercise = its volume / its repetitions; workout intensity = total volume / total lifts, \"the average weight lifted.\"\n- **Relative intensity** = intensity / 1RM x 100, \"usually more meaningful.\"\n- Exclusions: \"Some authorities (Chernyak, 1978) stipulate that only weights of greater than 40% of 1RM should be taken into account ... since the strengthening effects of lighter weights than this is minimal.\" Then: \"Generally, except for beginners, warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included in the calculations.\"\n- Near-misses count: \"if a weightlifter manages to raise a snatch or jerk above the head, but fails to complete the lift, then the attempt is counted\" (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324).\n- Hybrid exercises (e.g. 100 kg power clean x2 + front squat x3 + push press x1) are written as 100 over 2+3+1, volume 600 kg (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, verified against the page image).\n- Some, such as Medvedev (1989), prefer number of lifts alone, since computed averages let \"a few sets of lighter loads ... swamp the effect of a small number of maximal efforts\" (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' warning about averages: \"the use of overall averages can be very misleading in prescribing training, since they give no indication of the range of loads used ... Thus, the overall demands of training must always be examined simultaneously in terms of intensity and volume\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323).\n\n**When to apply** — Any weight-room load log or dashboard that reports tonnage or average load. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)); the baseline adds an Inference that \"with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes.\" Inference: the same caveat applies to relative intensity computed here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Two exclusion thresholds sit on the same page and are not reconciled: Chernyak's \"greater than 40% of 1RM\" and the general \"less than about 70% of 1RM\" warm-up exclusion; the worked example drops the 60 kg (50%) set as a warm-up but keeps the 80 kg set, which is 66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM. Inference from the printed numbers: the example therefore follows neither the 70% warm-up rule (which would also drop the 80 kg set) nor Chernyak's 40% rule (which would keep the 60 kg set) (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323, verified against the page image). Record which rule a log uses. The method covers barbell lifts; the authors log jumps separately by height and repetitions (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325). Small-muscle exercises are usually excluded, but the authors warn this can hide fatigue (PDF p. 340; see [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Exercise A, 60 kg x 10, 80 kg x 8, 100 kg x 4, 110 kg x 2, 1RM 120 kg. The 60 kg set (50%) is dropped as a warm-up. Volume = 640 + 400 + 220 = 1260 kg; reps = 14; intensity = 1260 / 14 = 90 kg; relative intensity = 90 / 120 x 100 = 75%. Yet the top set was 110 kg (92%), and \"The same relative intensity could be achieved by executing 3 identical sets each with 75kg for 3 repetitions,\" which \"would be far less demanding\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323). Inference: 3 x 3 at 75 kg is 62.5% of 120 kg, not 75%; the sentence only works if it means 90 kg, so treat that comparison as a printing slip.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/heuristic--intensity-defines-target-volume-defines-depth]]\n=== framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md\n\n# Cybernetic Periodisation: Steer the Preplanned Scheme With RPE and a Rating of Technique\n\n**What it is** — \"The use of numerical computations as the sole descriptor of loading often overlooks ... the athlete's *subjective perception of the intensity and overall effects of the loading*.\" Bulgarian weightlifting answered this with a method \"which required athletes always to train at a perceived maximum load\" (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328, verified against the page image). The proposal:\n- Add an RPE column (\"Rating of Perceived Effort\") on a 1-3 (light, medium, heavy), 1-10, or \"most popular\" 1-5 scale. \"You do not record an RPE for every repetition or exercise, but note it mentally in each set and use it to guide you in your next set. At the end of the workout, you then record only the RPE for the heaviest or most demanding sets.\"\n- RPE \"usually be used to identify any decrease rather than an increase in actual load.\"\n- Add a coach-scored Rating of Technique (RT) for the most demanding efforts, with video if more validity is needed (Table 6.4).\n- Plot intensities, volumes, actual performances and RPEs on the same axes over time.\n\n\"This modified form of periodisation may be known as *cybernetic periodisation*, since the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback obtained from the lifter's current performance state\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, verified against the page image), after Wiener (1948). \"Traditional periodisation can often prove to be as limited as non-periodised training, since all loading is based upon a fixed original input.\" It is \"a quantified extension of what may be termed intuitive training.\" The Bulgarian \"method of maximum effort\": if the top lift \"felt very demanding, the lifter might decrease the load by 10kg and perform a few sets of no more than 2 repetitions with it, and then follow this with an increase of 5kg for another few single attempts.\" Two side rules: periodically include minor exercises (calf raises, elbow curls, lat pulldowns) in calculations, since excluding them \"might disguise the fact that they are fatiguing some muscle groups,\" and avoid placing them randomly before major lifts; and use RPE and RT to adjust for heavy loading from the athlete's job, \"most significant in the case of heavy manual labourers\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the athlete responds to any exercise in terms of how the load feels at any given moment or period, rather than mechanically according to some numerical computation\" (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328); and \"Over-reliance on numerical computations ... is a major reason why some coaches tend to dismiss their relevance\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329). Voice: pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author; they address the reader as \"you\" and name neither Siff nor Verkhoshansky. Section 6.3, written in Siff's voice, refers back to it as among the \"methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" that \"this textbook introduced\" and points to \"the section on Cybernetic Periodisation\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334, verified against the page image). Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\n\n**When to apply** — Whenever a percentage-based plan runs on projected rather than tested maxes. Eric's baseline: Prilepin's chart is the default rep/set guide, with the baseline's own Inference that \"with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: RPE-guided downward adjustment is the authors' safeguard for exactly that case. For hard practice days in football, the \"normal daily job\" point applies by analogy to practice load before a lift (Inference).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors advise RPE mainly to reduce load, not to push it up. The 10 kg down, 5 kg up rule is a Bulgarian elite weightlifting example, not a general prescription. This entry overlaps with the authors' APRE in 5.2.2, which adjusts load by repetitions achieved rather than by perception ([[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]); the two are compatible but separate tools. Issurin maps workout load onto Borg RPE bands on a different scale ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]); do not mix the scales.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a Bulgarian lifter whose maximum attempt feels very demanding drops 10 kg for a few sets of at most 2 reps, then adds 5 kg for a few singles (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]], [[heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability]], [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"572b08f5-b10b-4c4d-b588-78c1f1e364c2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:20.545Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson.md\n\n# Early History of Periodisation: From Kotov's Three Stages to Dyson's Five Phases\n\n**What it is** — The authors trace training organisation from ancient practice to the first formal periodisation schemes (PDF p. 324-325 / printed p. 313-314, verified against the page images):\n- Ancient Greeks prepared for the Olympic Games with \"a preparatory training period of at least 10 months a year\" (PDF p. 324 / printed p. 313).\n- Kotov (*Olympic Sport*, 1917) divided training into general, preparatory and specific stages: the general stage developed the \"vegetative (cardiovascular-respiratory) and muscular systems\"; preparatory training of \"about two months' duration\" improved strength and muscle endurance; the specific stage had initial and main parts \"both of about four months' duration,\" but \"there was still no overall methodology of periodisation in the form of definite cycles\" (PDF p. 324 / printed p. 313).\n- Gorinewsky (1922) and Birsin (1925) took \"preliminary steps\"; sport-specific applications followed in 1938-1940 (track and field, skiing, gymnastics, boxing, water sports).\n- Pikhala (*Athletism*, Finland, 1930) used \"an undulating rhythm of training which alternates periods of work and rest\" to prevent overtraining, but planned around \"climatic seasons, rather than quantitatively determined phases of loading.\"\n- Grantyn (1939) split the year into preparatory, main and transitional periods, the transition being \"a gradual detraining to rest, followed by active rest\" with other sports such as gymnastics and jogging, which the authors call an early form of \"combinational training\" as restoration.\n- Ozolin (1949) stressed the competition calendar and climate, and active rest within the transition phase.\n- Letunov (1950) \"strongly criticised the formulation of training regimes on the basis of the competition calendar alone,\" holding that stages follow biological processes, though \"he failed to appreciate that these processes, in turn, were modified by the training loads\" (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n- Dyson (*A New System of Training*, England, 1946) gave a five-phase athletics year: non-competitive preparation (5 months), pre-competitive (1 month), initial competitive (6 weeks), main competitive (6 weeks), post-competitive (6 weeks) (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Why it works** — This is a historical account, not a mechanism. The authors' through-line is that organisation moved from seasons and calendars toward phases set by biological adaptation, and they fault Letunov for missing that the loads themselves change those biological processes (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**When to apply** — When explaining to staff or students where macro, meso and microcycle language came from, or when an older Russian or British source uses phase names that do not match current terms. Eric's baseline: \"Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 179, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence that any one historical scheme works: the authors give no outcome data for Kotov, Grantyn or Dyson. Pikhala's \"undulating rhythm\" (1930) is about alternating work and rest, and is not the same thing as the modern undulating (daily or weekly varied) resistance model; Inference: do not cite it as an origin of that model without checking Pikhala. The authors themselves warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases, that this history \"may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation,\" which they call \"far from the truth\" (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Dyson's 1946 athletics year, 5 months of non-competitive preparation with \"gym training and cross-country running,\" then 1 month of pre-competitive specific preparation, then three 6-week blocks (initial competitive, main competitive, post-competitive) (PDF p. 325 / printed p. 314).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--seasonal-trainability-hettinger-muller-ultraviolet]], [[framework--sport-alone-vs-sport-plus-supplementary-training-and-adjunct-modes]], [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--evans-history-lineage-of-sport-science-giants]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n=== heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability.md\n\n# Track Reliability of Technique, and Progress Only When It Has Stabilised at 70-80% or Better\n\n**What it is** — \"Even the most meticulous computation and prescription of load intensity, volume and sequencing ... is of little consequence to performance if changes in technical mastery are not constantly monitored.\" Lab tests \"simply identify the potential offered by the athlete's work capacity and not functional preparedness\" (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318, verified against the page image). The tool is *reliability of technique*: the percentage of successes in executing the same specific skills, kept separately for training and competition. \"A low competitive reliability relative to training reliability indicates that the athlete probably has difficulty in performing under conditions of high emotional excitement and needs to simulate those conditions during training\"; \"gym lifters\" lift much more in training than on the platform, where emotional stress affects \"not ... the obviously adequate levels of strength or power, but ... the technical skills required.\" Rule: \"Progression to more difficult skills should not occur until the reliability at the preceding level of complexity or intensity has stabilised at a level of at least 70-80%,\" the \"*zone-by-zone mastering of intensity* (Matveyev, 1977).\" Technical-training dose is steered by a *feedforward method* (\"the quantitatively planned long-term scheme\") and a *feedback method* (\"the ad hoc daily approach\") because technical emphasis \"can fluctuate rapidly\" (PDF p. 330 / printed p. 319, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: performance depends on \"functional preparedness, which relies on the mastery of specific motor skills,\" and \"loss of form is prematurely attributed to overtraining or deficient training\" when the real problem is inadequate monitoring and cyclical variation of skills training (PDF p. 329-330 / printed p. 318-319).\n\n**When to apply** — Any skill progression with a clear success criterion (a lift made, a drill rep executed to standard). Inference: `docs/method-baseline.md` records no explicit technique-reliability metric, so this is a gap the passage could fill. The authors later add a coach-scored Rating of Technique column to the session log (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328; see [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 70-80% threshold has no cited data beyond the Matveyev label, and it needs \"zones of intensity or complexity\" to be \"formally laid down for each aspect of a given sport\" first. Inference: in open, reactive team-sport skills a success rate is harder to define than in a weightlifting attempt, so the threshold transfers best to closed skills. Inference: the second bullet in the source list (\"the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification\", PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318) reads as a factor to avoid, not one to stress; the source wording is ambiguous.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the \"gym lifter\" whose training lifts far exceed platform lifts, read as a technical and emotional problem rather than a strength deficit (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]], [[framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model]], [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]\n=== framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation.md\n\n# Calculating Volume, Mean Intensity and Relative Intensity, and Why Averages Mislead\n\n**What it is** — Volume is \"intended to provide some measure of the total work done over a given period and intensity is a measure of the average force exerted\"; approximate measures replace \"the precise methods of physics\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323, verified against the page image).\n- **Volume** of an exercise (kg) = weight x repetitions; workout volume = sum over \"all the major exercises.\"\n- **Number of lifts** = total repetitions.\n- **Intensity** of an exercise = its volume / its repetitions; workout intensity = total volume / total lifts, \"the average weight lifted.\"\n- **Relative intensity** = intensity / 1RM x 100, \"usually more meaningful.\"\n- Exclusions: \"Some authorities (Chernyak, 1978) stipulate that only weights of greater than 40% of 1RM should be taken into account ... since the strengthening effects of lighter weights than this is minimal.\" Then: \"Generally, except for beginners, warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included in the calculations.\"\n- Near-misses count: \"if a weightlifter manages to raise a snatch or jerk above the head, but fails to complete the lift, then the attempt is counted\" (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324).\n- Hybrid exercises (e.g. 100 kg power clean x2 + front squat x3 + push press x1) are written as 100 over 2+3+1, volume 600 kg (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, verified against the page image).\n- Some, such as Medvedev (1989), prefer number of lifts alone, since computed averages let \"a few sets of lighter loads ... swamp the effect of a small number of maximal efforts\" (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' warning about averages: \"the use of overall averages can be very misleading in prescribing training, since they give no indication of the range of loads used ... Thus, the overall demands of training must always be examined simultaneously in terms of intensity and volume\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323).\n\n**When to apply** — Any weight-room load log or dashboard that reports tonnage or average load. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)); the baseline adds an Inference that \"with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes.\" Inference: the same caveat applies to relative intensity computed here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Two exclusion thresholds sit on the same page and are not reconciled: Chernyak's \"greater than 40% of 1RM\" and the general \"less than about 70% of 1RM\" warm-up exclusion; the worked example drops the 60 kg (50%) set as a warm-up but keeps the 80 kg set, which is 66.7% of the 120 kg 1RM. Inference from the printed numbers: the example therefore follows neither the 70% warm-up rule (which would also drop the 80 kg set) nor Chernyak's 40% rule (which would keep the 60 kg set) (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323, verified against the page image). Record which rule a log uses. The method covers barbell lifts; the authors log jumps separately by height and repetitions (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325). Small-muscle exercises are usually excluded, but the authors warn this can hide fatigue (PDF p. 340; see [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Exercise A, 60 kg x 10, 80 kg x 8, 100 kg x 4, 110 kg x 2, 1RM 120 kg. The 60 kg set (50%) is dropped as a warm-up. Volume = 640 + 400 + 220 = 1260 kg; reps = 14; intensity = 1260 / 14 = 90 kg; relative intensity = 90 / 120 x 100 = 75%. Yet the top set was 110 kg (92%), and \"The same relative intensity could be achieved by executing 3 identical sets each with 75kg for 3 repetitions,\" which \"would be far less demanding\" (PDF p. 334 / printed p. 323). Inference: 3 x 3 at 75 kg is 62.5% of 120 kg, not 75%; the sentence only works if it means 90 kg, so treat that comparison as a printing slip.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/heuristic--intensity-defines-target-volume-defines-depth]]\n=== framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique.md\n\n# Cybernetic Periodisation: Steer the Preplanned Scheme With RPE and a Rating of Technique\n\n**What it is** — \"The use of numerical computations as the sole descriptor of loading often overlooks ... the athlete's *subjective perception of the intensity and overall effects of the loading*.\" Bulgarian weightlifting answered this with a method \"which required athletes always to train at a perceived maximum load\" (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328, verified against the page image). The proposal:\n- Add an RPE column (\"Rating of Perceived Effort\") on a 1-3 (light, medium, heavy), 1-10, or \"most popular\" 1-5 scale. \"You do not record an RPE for every repetition or exercise, but note it mentally in each set and use it to guide you in your next set. At the end of the workout, you then record only the RPE for the heaviest or most demanding sets.\"\n- RPE \"usually be used to identify any decrease rather than an increase in actual load.\"\n- Add a coach-scored Rating of Technique (RT) for the most demanding efforts, with video if more validity is needed (Table 6.4).\n- Plot intensities, volumes, actual performances and RPEs on the same axes over time.\n\n\"This modified form of periodisation may be known as *cybernetic periodisation*, since the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback obtained from the lifter's current performance state\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, verified against the page image), after Wiener (1948). \"Traditional periodisation can often prove to be as limited as non-periodised training, since all loading is based upon a fixed original input.\" It is \"a quantified extension of what may be termed intuitive training.\" The Bulgarian \"method of maximum effort\": if the top lift \"felt very demanding, the lifter might decrease the load by 10kg and perform a few sets of no more than 2 repetitions with it, and then follow this with an increase of 5kg for another few single attempts.\" Two side rules: periodically include minor exercises (calf raises, elbow curls, lat pulldowns) in calculations, since excluding them \"might disguise the fact that they are fatiguing some muscle groups,\" and avoid placing them randomly before major lifts; and use RPE and RT to adjust for heavy loading from the athlete's job, \"most significant in the case of heavy manual labourers\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the athlete responds to any exercise in terms of how the load feels at any given moment or period, rather than mechanically according to some numerical computation\" (PDF p. 339 / printed p. 328); and \"Over-reliance on numerical computations ... is a major reason why some coaches tend to dismiss their relevance\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329). Voice: pages 339-340 do not attribute the method to either author; they address the reader as \"you\" and name neither Siff nor Verkhoshansky. Section 6.3, written in Siff's voice, refers back to it as among the \"methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" that \"this textbook introduced\" and points to \"the section on Cybernetic Periodisation\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334, verified against the page image). Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\n\n**When to apply** — Whenever a percentage-based plan runs on projected rather than tested maxes. Eric's baseline: Prilepin's chart is the default rep/set guide, with the baseline's own Inference that \"with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: RPE-guided downward adjustment is the authors' safeguard for exactly that case. For hard practice days in football, the \"normal daily job\" point applies by analogy to practice load before a lift (Inference).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors advise RPE mainly to reduce load, not to push it up. The 10 kg down, 5 kg up rule is a Bulgarian elite weightlifting example, not a general prescription. This entry overlaps with the authors' APRE in 5.2.2, which adjusts load by repetitions achieved rather than by perception ([[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]); the two are compatible but separate tools. Issurin maps workout load onto Borg RPE bands on a different scale ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]); do not mix the scales.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a Bulgarian lifter whose maximum attempt feels very demanding drops 10 kg for a few sets of at most 2 reps, then adds 5 kg for a few singles (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]], [[heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability]], [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"342856e3-e8b7-4cbf-916b-ef51edd5f875","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
