{"parentUuid":"261dc7ee-ab49-44e3-a832-8db48a7a57e1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo2WMVVNsUQoqC1eKvF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four smaller fixes land correctly. Now spot checks on GO entries: Table 6.1, Table 6.2, Chernyak, the Vorobyev figures and the readiness-markers entry."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1463,"cache_read_input_tokens":106209,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1463,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffo2SQdH9Zau6pvthLe7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"00130bfd-c159-4303-8fdb-34eafae0f942","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:38.144Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"00130bfd-c159-4303-8fdb-34eafae0f942","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo2WMVVNsUQoqC1eKvF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FDYBPzZ9gbvud48ThxK8Lf","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification.md claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations.md framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '9p;15,$p' $f; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1463,"cache_read_input_tokens":106209,"output_tokens":554,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":257},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1463},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":554,"cache_read_input_tokens":106209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1463,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1463,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FDYBPzZ9gbvud48ThxK8Lf":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification.md claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations.md framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '9p;15,$p' $f; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffo2SQdH9Zau6pvthLe7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4190eecd-86af-4d30-bbed-1938f305d10e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:39.675Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4190eecd-86af-4d30-bbed-1938f305d10e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FDYBPzZ9gbvud48ThxK8Lf","type":"tool_result","content":"=== claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification.md\nconfidence: supported\n\n**What it is** — For Olympic weightlifters, \"based on research and experience with Russian lifters (Vorobyev, 1985),\" the authors say \"There is an optimal mean training weight in each bodymass division for each level of qualification (Table 6.2).\" Two sentences later the same table \"furnishes upper limits of the mean intensity which the speed-strength athlete should not exceed over any cycle of training\" (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, verified against the page image). The values rise with qualification and bodymass, for example (mean training load, kg; Class III / Class II / Class I / CMS / MS):\n- 52 kg: 51.0 / 63.5 / 70.0 / 78.0 / 80.0\n- 75 kg: 70.0 / 92.0 / 99.0 / 111.0 / 116.0\n- 100 kg: 82.0 / 101.0 / 113.0 / 125.0 / 132.0\n- +110 kg: 86.0 / 109.0 / 120.0 / 130.0 / 147.0\n\n(CMS = Candidate for Master of Sport, MS = Master of Sport.) The authors say the values rise as human strength increases, adjustable \"by using the formula presented in Section 3.3.9.\" For juniors, Dvorkin's research (1980), using a maximum and recovery heart rate method, gives \"fundamental training weights\" of \"50-70%\" for 13-14 year olds, \"60-90%\" for 15-16 year olds and \"80-90%\" for 17-20 year olds, \"the most suitable value depending on individual responses to the loading (Medvedev, 1986).\"\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism; the table summarises Russian research and experience. The authors note that it \"does not reflect the distribution of loading over the different intensity zones.\"\n\n**When to apply** — As a reference for how much mean training load scales with qualification inside one bodymass class, and as an argument for setting load caps by level rather than by a single percentage. Eric's baseline: Prilepin's chart as the default rep/set guide (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: Table 6.2 is in absolute kilograms for weightlifting classes, so it does not convert to Baylor athletes; its use is the principle that mean load caps rise with qualification.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The table is introduced as both an \"optimal\" mean and an \"upper limit\" not to exceed; the source does not reconcile the two readings, so do not treat the numbers as targets. The junior percentages have no stated reference (percent of what maximum) on the page; Inference: probably of 1RM. Bondarchuk reports that model characteristics by qualification exist and are published in tables ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]]); that agrees in kind but is a separate data set.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own table: a 75 kg Class II lifter's mean training load is 92.0 kg, a 75 kg Master of Sport's is 116.0 kg (Table 6.2, PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording]], [[claim--strength-age-relationship-and-adjustment-equations]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]]\n=== claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations.md\nconfidence: contested\n\n**What it is** — \"Studies have shown that, in the annual macrocycle, the weekly levels of volume and intensity coincide fairly closely in sports requiring strength (Chernyak et al, 1979). Generally, the largest number of weeks involve medium volume and medium intensity, as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity.\" Chernyak's recommendations for strength-speed sports (PDF p. 343 / printed p. 332, verified against the page image):\n1. \"It is inadvisable always to combine low volume with low intensity, medium volume with medium intensity and large volume with high intensity.\"\n2. \"Training is most effective and least injurious if\": medium volume, medium intensity \"used most frequently (about 12-15 weeks per year)\"; medium or high volume, low intensity next (9-10 weeks); low volume, medium intensity next (6-7 weeks).\n3. Less often still: large volume, medium intensity (3-4 weeks); large volume, high intensity (2-3 weeks); low volume, high intensity (3-4 weeks); low volume, low intensity (1-2 weeks).\n\nThe authors add that overall volume and intensity \"tell us little about the loading imposed in different intensity zones or at the level of individual muscle groups,\" nor about \"the individual's perceived response,\" so attention must go to these and to \"the level of mastery of the athlete\" (PDF p. 343-344 / printed p. 332-333).\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism is given; the basis is Chernyak's research, summarised without data. Inference: rule 1's word \"always\" may mean \"do not tie volume to intensity in lockstep,\" which would make it a rule against rigid coupling rather than against medium/medium weeks.\n\n**When to apply** — As a rough annual budget of week types for strength-speed athletes: mostly medium/medium, then higher-volume low-intensity, then low-volume medium-intensity, with high-intensity weeks rare. Eric's baseline: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 181, Inferred (transcript)), and \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Chernyak's table gives a yearly frequency check on such waves.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book, same page, same question.** (a) The opening says the most frequent weeks are medium/medium \"as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity,\" but recommendation 3 puts large volume with high intensity at only 2-3 weeks a year. (b) Recommendation 1 calls it inadvisable to \"always\" combine medium volume with medium intensity, while recommendation 2 makes medium/medium the most frequent week (12-15 weeks). One reading of (a) is that the opening describes observed practice and the recommendations prescribe change, but the text does not say so (Inference). Inference: the week ranges sum to about 36-45 weeks, not 52, so the remaining weeks are unaccounted for. No definitions of \"low,\" \"medium,\" \"high\" or \"large\" are given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: about 12-15 medium/medium weeks, 9-10 medium-or-high volume with low intensity, 6-7 low volume with medium intensity, and only 2-3 large volume with high intensity in a strength-speed athlete's year (PDF p. 343 / printed p. 332).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording]], [[claim--heavy-medium-light-alternation-beats-monotonic-increasing-loading]], [[framework--eight-weaknesses-of-the-matveyev-model]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/heuristic--intensity-defines-target-volume-defines-depth]]\n=== framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording.md\nconfidence: supported\n\n**What it is** — Because averages hide the range of loads, \"it is preferable to determine the distribution of loading in different intensity zones. In strength sports, nine intensity zones are usually defined, although it is adequate to use the following five zones: 50%+ (50-59%), 60%+ (between 60-69%), 70%+ (70-79%), 80%+ (80-89%), 90%+ (90-100%).\" The number or percentage of lifts in each zone is recorded with volume and intensity (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324, verified against the page image). Fig 6.7 (based on Matveyev 1977) plots number of lifts as a percentage of monthly volume across zones I-V: A, a \"normal\" distribution peaking at zone III at about 40%; B, skewed toward lower intensity, peaking at about 40% around zone II; C, skewed toward higher intensity, peaking at about 40% around zone IV (values read by eye). The training diary puts exercises, sets, reps and load on the left page and calculations on the right (Table 6.1: zone columns I-V, then Vol, NL, Iav, 1RM, Irel). These figures are recorded \"at the level of the individual session, the week or microcycle, the month, the mesocycle and the macrocycle.\" Three notations for sets are given, e.g. \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" or \"10(60)1, 8(70)1, 6(80)2\" (PDF p. 335-336 / printed p. 324-325).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that zone counts give \"a clear visual overview of how the loading is imposed,\" which volume and mean intensity cannot (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324). They add a caution, not a mechanism: \"The mistake should not be made of assuming that higher intensities (mean loads) or a greater number of lifts in the higher intensity zones will produce superior results. At any given time, there is an optimal load and load distribution for athletes of different bodymass and level of qualification, as well as individual responses to different loading regimes.\"\n\n**When to apply** — When building weight-room reports, report lifts per zone per week alongside tonnage. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: Prilepin's chart is itself organised by intensity zones, so zone counts are the natural way to check a block against it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Zone I is defined two ways on the same page: the text says \"50%+ (50-59%),\" while Table 6.1's column I is headed \"60%-\" (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324, image). Pick one and state it. Zone counts depend on a current 1RM; without true 1RM testing they rest on projected maxes (the Inference attached to baseline line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). The authors give no target distribution; Fig 6.7 shows shapes, not prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a week of 60 squat lifts with 12 in zone III, 30 in zone IV and 18 in zone V would plot as a Fig 6.7 type C (skewed high) distribution; the authors would warn against assuming that is better than type A.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f243203e-20c5-4f83-822e-aa1c04937f45","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:39.897Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification.md\nconfidence: supported\n\n**What it is** — For Olympic weightlifters, \"based on research and experience with Russian lifters (Vorobyev, 1985),\" the authors say \"There is an optimal mean training weight in each bodymass division for each level of qualification (Table 6.2).\" Two sentences later the same table \"furnishes upper limits of the mean intensity which the speed-strength athlete should not exceed over any cycle of training\" (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, verified against the page image). The values rise with qualification and bodymass, for example (mean training load, kg; Class III / Class II / Class I / CMS / MS):\n- 52 kg: 51.0 / 63.5 / 70.0 / 78.0 / 80.0\n- 75 kg: 70.0 / 92.0 / 99.0 / 111.0 / 116.0\n- 100 kg: 82.0 / 101.0 / 113.0 / 125.0 / 132.0\n- +110 kg: 86.0 / 109.0 / 120.0 / 130.0 / 147.0\n\n(CMS = Candidate for Master of Sport, MS = Master of Sport.) The authors say the values rise as human strength increases, adjustable \"by using the formula presented in Section 3.3.9.\" For juniors, Dvorkin's research (1980), using a maximum and recovery heart rate method, gives \"fundamental training weights\" of \"50-70%\" for 13-14 year olds, \"60-90%\" for 15-16 year olds and \"80-90%\" for 17-20 year olds, \"the most suitable value depending on individual responses to the loading (Medvedev, 1986).\"\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism; the table summarises Russian research and experience. The authors note that it \"does not reflect the distribution of loading over the different intensity zones.\"\n\n**When to apply** — As a reference for how much mean training load scales with qualification inside one bodymass class, and as an argument for setting load caps by level rather than by a single percentage. Eric's baseline: Prilepin's chart as the default rep/set guide (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: Table 6.2 is in absolute kilograms for weightlifting classes, so it does not convert to Baylor athletes; its use is the principle that mean load caps rise with qualification.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The table is introduced as both an \"optimal\" mean and an \"upper limit\" not to exceed; the source does not reconcile the two readings, so do not treat the numbers as targets. The junior percentages have no stated reference (percent of what maximum) on the page; Inference: probably of 1RM. Bondarchuk reports that model characteristics by qualification exist and are published in tables ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]]); that agrees in kind but is a separate data set.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own table: a 75 kg Class II lifter's mean training load is 92.0 kg, a 75 kg Master of Sport's is 116.0 kg (Table 6.2, PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording]], [[claim--strength-age-relationship-and-adjustment-equations]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]]\n=== claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations.md\nconfidence: contested\n\n**What it is** — \"Studies have shown that, in the annual macrocycle, the weekly levels of volume and intensity coincide fairly closely in sports requiring strength (Chernyak et al, 1979). Generally, the largest number of weeks involve medium volume and medium intensity, as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity.\" Chernyak's recommendations for strength-speed sports (PDF p. 343 / printed p. 332, verified against the page image):\n1. \"It is inadvisable always to combine low volume with low intensity, medium volume with medium intensity and large volume with high intensity.\"\n2. \"Training is most effective and least injurious if\": medium volume, medium intensity \"used most frequently (about 12-15 weeks per year)\"; medium or high volume, low intensity next (9-10 weeks); low volume, medium intensity next (6-7 weeks).\n3. Less often still: large volume, medium intensity (3-4 weeks); large volume, high intensity (2-3 weeks); low volume, high intensity (3-4 weeks); low volume, low intensity (1-2 weeks).\n\nThe authors add that overall volume and intensity \"tell us little about the loading imposed in different intensity zones or at the level of individual muscle groups,\" nor about \"the individual's perceived response,\" so attention must go to these and to \"the level of mastery of the athlete\" (PDF p. 343-344 / printed p. 332-333).\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism is given; the basis is Chernyak's research, summarised without data. Inference: rule 1's word \"always\" may mean \"do not tie volume to intensity in lockstep,\" which would make it a rule against rigid coupling rather than against medium/medium weeks.\n\n**When to apply** — As a rough annual budget of week types for strength-speed athletes: mostly medium/medium, then higher-volume low-intensity, then low-volume medium-intensity, with high-intensity weeks