loading in different intensity zones. In strength sports, nine intensity zones are usually defined, although it is adequate to use the following five zones: 50%+ (50-59%), 60%+ (between 60-69%), 70%+ (70-79%), 80%+ (80-89%), 90%+ (90-100%).\" The number or percentage of lifts in each zone is recorded with volume and intensity (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324, verified against the page image). Fig 6.7 (based on Matveyev 1977) plots number of lifts as a percentage of monthly volume across zones I-V: A, a \"normal\" distribution peaking at zone III at about 40%; B, skewed toward lower intensity, peaking at about 40% around zone II; C, skewed toward higher intensity, peaking at about 40% around zone IV (values read by eye). The training diary puts exercises, sets, reps and load on the left page and calculations on the right (Table 6.1: zone columns I-V, then Vol, NL, Iav, 1RM, Irel). These figures are recorded \"at the level of the individual session, the week or microcycle, the month, the mesocycle and the macrocycle.\" Three notations for sets are given, e.g. \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" or \"10(60)1, 8(70)1, 6(80)2\" (PDF p. 335-336 / printed p. 324-325).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that zone counts give \"a clear visual overview of how the loading is imposed,\" which volume and mean intensity cannot (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324). They add a caution, not a mechanism: \"The mistake should not be made of assuming that higher intensities (mean loads) or a greater number of lifts in the higher intensity zones will produce superior results. At any given time, there is an optimal load and load distribution for athletes of different bodymass and level of qualification, as well as individual responses to different loading regimes.\"\n\n**When to apply** — When building weight-room reports, report lifts per zone per week alongside tonnage. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: Prilepin's chart is itself organised by intensity zones, so zone counts are the natural way to check a block against it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Zone I is defined two ways on the same page: the text says \"50%+ (50-59%),\" while Table 6.1's column I is headed \"60%-\" (PDF p. 335 / printed p. 324, image). Pick one and state it. Zone counts depend on a current 1RM; without true 1RM testing they rest on projected maxes (the Inference attached to baseline line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). The authors give no target distribution; Fig 6.7 shows shapes, not prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a week of 60 squat lifts with 12 in zone III, 30 in zone IV and 18 in zone V would plot as a Fig 6.7 type C (skewed high) distribution; the authors would warn against assuming that is better than type A.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation]], [[claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification]], [[framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4190eecd-86af-4d30-bbed-1938f305d10e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
