:"assistant","uuid":"e54d7795-30f2-4173-a533-12239e16e262","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:53.736Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e54d7795-30f2-4173-a533-12239e16e262","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n16:**What it is** — Figures illustrating step-like load alternation in weightlifting (PDF p. 333-334 / printed p. 322-323, all verified against the page images; the authors refer to them as \"Figs 6.2-6.6\" under scheme 2, PDF p. 332):\n17:- **Daily load within a week, qualified weightlifter** (captioned \"Figure 6.2,\" PDF p. 333; this is a second figure with that number, distinct from the Ryun figure on PDF p. 332). A, \"the average week a few months before competitions\": day 1 42%, day 2 23%, day 3 35% of the weekly load. B, \"the week directly before a contest\": 54%, 30%, 16%. Printed values.\n18:- **Weekly volume within a month** (Fig 6.3, Vorobyev 1978). A, \"highly qualified lifter in the month before a contest\": weeks 1-4 at 15%, 23%, 37%, 25% of the monthly load. B, \"if the previous month volume of loading was high\": 13%, 21%, 30%, 36%. Printed values.\n19:- **Two months, qualified weightlifters** (Fig 6.4, Ermakov 1974): weeks labelled L, M, L, S, L, M, ML, S; \"shown to be more effective than traditional schemes which gradually increase, then decrease the loading volume.\"\n20:- **Two months, elite weightlifters** (Fig 6.5, Vorobyev 1978): L, M, L, S, M, L, M, L; \"more effective than that of Figure 6.3, since world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads.\"\n21:- **Two months, well trained lifters \"who can tolerate two successive weeks of heavy loading\"** (Fig 6.6, Vorobyev 1978): L, L, S, M, L, L, S, M.\n23:Figs 6.4-6.6 carry letter labels only, no percentages, and the book does not define the letters.\n25:**Why it works** — The authors place these under scheme 2, whose success they say \"has been corroborated\" by Yakovlev, Grokin, Vorobyev and Ermakov (PDF p. 332 / printed p. 321). No mechanism is given; the captions' reason for Fig 6.5 is that \"world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads.\"\n27:**When to apply** — As concrete reference shapes when writing a weekly or monthly load wave for strength-power athletes, especially the pre-contest week (Fig 6.2B: front-loaded, falling to 16% on the last day). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Fig 6.2B's falling three-day pattern is the same front-loaded logic in a weightlifting week.\n29:**When NOT to apply** — Weightlifting only, and the population detail (\"qualified,\" \"highly qualified,\" \"elite,\" \"well trained\") is all the page gives; there are no sample sizes or outcome data in the figures, so `untested`. Two oddities to keep visible: Fig 6.3B, for when \"the previous month volume of loading was high,\" still rises to its largest week (36%) at week 4, and Fig 6.5's caption compares it with Fig 6.3, a one-month pre-contest scheme, rather than with Fig 6.4. Inference: the weekly shares within a month and the day shares within a week are proportions, so they say nothing about absolute volume.\n31:**Example** — The authors' own: in the week before a contest (Fig 6.2B), 54% of the weekly load on day 1, 30% on day 2, 16% on day 3 (PDF p. 333 / printed p. 322).\n(eval):1: == not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_017QuFSfMLYCPzCvMM3kHEXi"}]},"uuid":"ffb4486d-9629-424e-b8b7-5b3a7599fbe4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:53.844Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n16:**What it is** — Figures illustrating step-like load alternation in weightlifting (PDF p. 333-334 / printed p. 322-323, all verified against the page images; the authors refer to them as \"Figs 6.2-6.6\" under scheme 2, PDF p. 332):\n17:- **Daily load within a week, qualified weightlifter** (captioned \"Figure 6.2,\" PDF p. 333; this is a second figure with that number, distinct from the Ryun figure on PDF p. 332). A, \"the average week a few months before competitions\": day 1 42%, day 2 23%, day 3 35% of the weekly load. B, \"the week directly before a contest\": 54%, 30%, 16%. Printed values.\n18:- **Weekly volume within a month** (Fig 6.3, Vorobyev 1978). A, \"highly qualified lifter in the month before a contest\": weeks 1-4 at 15%, 23%, 37%, 25% of the monthly load. B, \"if the previous month volume of loading was high\": 13%, 21%, 30%, 36%. Printed values.\n19:- **Two months, qualified weightlifters** (Fig 6.4, Ermakov 1974): weeks labelled L, M, L, S, L, M, ML, S; \"shown to be more effective than traditional schemes which gradually increase, then decrease the loading volume.\"\n20:- **Two months, elite weightlifters** (Fig 6.5, Vorobyev 1978): L, M, L, S, M, L, M, L; \"more effective than that of Figure 6.3, since world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads.\"\n21:- **Two months, well trained lifters \"who can tolerate two successive weeks of heavy loading\"** (Fig 6.6, Vorobyev 1978): L, L, S, M, L, L, S, M.\n23:Figs 6.4-6.6 carry letter labels only, no percentages, and the book does not define the letters.\n25:**Why it works** — The authors place these under scheme 2, whose success they say \"has been corroborated\" by Yakovlev, Grokin, Vorobyev and Ermakov (PDF p. 332 / printed p. 321). No mechanism is given; the captions' reason for Fig 6.5 is that \"world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads.\"\n27:**When to apply** — As concrete reference shapes when writing a weekly or monthly load wave for strength-power athletes, especially the pre-contest week (Fig 6.2B: front-loaded, falling to 16% on the last day). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Fig 6.2B's falling three-day pattern is the same front-loaded logic in a weightlifting week.\n29:**When NOT to apply** — Weightlifting only, and the population detail (\"qualified,\" \"highly qualified,\" \"elite,\" \"well trained\") is all the page gives; there are no sample sizes or outcome data in the figures, so `untested`. Two oddities to keep visible: Fig 6.3B, for when \"the previous month volume of loading was high,\" still rises to its largest week (36%) at week 4, and Fig 6.5's caption compares it with Fig 6.3, a one-month pre-contest scheme, rather than with Fig 6.4. Inference: the weekly shares within a month and the day shares within a week are proportions, so they say nothing about absolute volume.\n31:**Example** — The authors' own: in the week before a contest (Fig 6.2B), 54% of the weekly load on day 1, 30% on day 2, 16% on day 3 (PDF p. 333 / printed p. 322).\n(eval):1: == not found","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e54d7795-30f2-4173-a533-12239e16e262","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
