{"parentUuid":"5cfe01fa-3899-4a89-9740-3e39805d4b61","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KiT2qk3xhQsDgSymWrxhHg","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — \"The *gradual overload principle*, with periodic underloading days or microcycles, is constantly adhered to through all training cycles to ensure that an adequate, but not excessive, training stimulus is imposed on the body. A preliminary recommendation is that individual workouts should generally be alternated in terms of light, medium and heavy loading to guard against overtraining and to ensure optimal training and competitive efficiency. This variation in training load on a daily, microcyclic and mesocyclic basis is essential for avoiding stagnation. In this respect, it is common to observe the well-known empirical *Rule of Sixty Percent*, which states that the training volume of any stage (day or microcycle) with light loading should be approximately 60% of the volume of a stage with heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading\" (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318, verified against the page image).\n\n and heavy loading to guard against overtraining and to ensure optimal training and competitive \nefficiency. This variation in training load on a daily, microcyclic and mesocyclic basis is essential for avoiding \nstagnation. In this respect, it is common to observe the well-known empirical Rule o f Sixty Percent, which states \nthat the training volume of any stage (day or microcycle) with light loading should be approximately 60% of the \nvolume of a stage with heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading.\nAcquisition and Stabilisation of Technical Skills\nEven the most meticulous computation and prescription of load intensity, volume and sequencing that optimally \ndevelops work capacity is of little conscqucnce to performance if changes in technical mastery are not constantly \nmonitored. Laboratory tests may reveal excellent maximal strength, VO2 max, muscle endurance, reaction times \nand so forth, but they simply identify the potential offered by the athlete's work capacity and not function","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"385ffe22-eb26-404b-bd8c-b8fd1f85f36b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:42:05.159Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — \"The *gradual overload principle*, with periodic underloading days or microcycles, is constantly adhered to through all training cycles to ensure that an adequate, but not excessive, training stimulus is imposed on the body. A preliminary recommendation is that individual workouts should generally be alternated in terms of light, medium and heavy loading to guard against overtraining and to ensure optimal training and competitive efficiency. This variation in training load on a daily, microcyclic and mesocyclic basis is essential for avoiding stagnation. In this respect, it is common to observe the well-known empirical *Rule of Sixty Percent*, which states that the training volume of any stage (day or microcycle) with light loading should be approximately 60% of the volume of a stage with heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading\" (PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318, verified against the page image).\n\n and heavy loading to guard against overtraining and to ensure optimal training and competitive \nefficiency. This variation in training load on a daily, microcyclic and mesocyclic basis is essential for avoiding \nstagnation. In this respect, it is common to observe the well-known empirical Rule o f Sixty Percent, which states \nthat the training volume of any stage (day or microcycle) with light loading should be approximately 60% of the \nvolume of a stage with heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading.\nAcquisition and Stabilisation of Technical Skills\nEven the most meticulous computation and prescription of load intensity, volume and sequencing that optimally \ndevelops work capacity is of little conscqucnce to performance if changes in technical mastery are not constantly \nmonitored. Laboratory tests may reveal excellent maximal strength, VO2 max, muscle endurance, reaction times \nand so forth, but they simply identify the potential offered by the athlete's work capacity and not function","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5cfe01fa-3899-4a89-9740-3e39805d4b61","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
