{"parentUuid":"47b78adb-f6f2-417c-af97-28b6e169c2ff","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo6ERACrJBsUBMUrLbF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All checks pass: wikilinks resolve, headers are correctly formatted, and no em dash or link issues—the \"history\" flags were false positives. Now writing the final report.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":585,"cache_read_input_tokens":134887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":585,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1,"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffo64y9DhMaZrsCuCzHi","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"17ac9d22-179f-4cf7-a59c-3e48ca785811","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:42:33.730Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"17ac9d22-179f-4cf7-a59c-3e48ca785811","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo6ERACrJBsUBMUrLbF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XNjetdCjjv7ja6VvthY87A","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 6a (6.1-6.3), 28 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11. Text pulled with PyMuPDF; images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_6a_p<N>.png` (plus crops `r2_6a_p345crop.png`, `r2_6a_p346crop.png`). Images opened: PDF 333 (Figs 6.2-6.5), 335 (Fig 6.7, Table 6.1), 336 (Table 6.2, notations), 341 (Table 6.5), 343 (Chernyak), and the PDF 345 and 346 crops (OCR-garbled quote words). Text checked for PDF 325, 329, 334, 339, 340, 344, 345, 346, 347.\nInputs read: `review_book_prompt.md`, `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, round-1 review `review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md`, fix log `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md`.\n\n## 1. Round-1 FIX entries (6)\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson | GO | Fix landed: \"warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\". PDF 325 / p.314 text: Dyson's five-phase list, then the heading \"Ways of Organising Training\", then \"may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation ... This is far from the truth\". Dyson 5 mo / 1 mo / 6 / 6 / 6 wk and \"gym training and cross-country running\" rechecked on PDF 325. No new error. | none |\n| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability | GO | Fix landed: \"the second bullet in the source list ('the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification', PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318)\". PDF 329 bullet order confirmed: (1) eliminating disruptive factors, (2) premature introduction of skills, (3) mental control. Quote verbatim. Labelled Inference. 70-80% and \"zone-by-zone mastering of intensity (Matveyev, 1977)\" rechecked. | none |\n| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | GO | Fix landed and correct. PDF 334 / p.323: Chernyak (1978) \">40% of 1RM\"; \"warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included\"; Exercise A 60x10, 80x8, 100x4, 110x2, 1RM 120; \"The first set with 60kg (i.e. 50% of 1RM) is ignored\"; Va = 640+400+220 = 1260; Ra = 14; Ia = 90; Irel = 75%. 80/120 = 66.7%, so the example follows neither rule; the arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed numbers\". The separate 62.5% printing-slip Inference (3x3 at 75 kg) is still correct. No new error. | none |\n| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | GO | Fix landed. PyMuPDF text of PDF 339 and 340 contains neither \"Siff\" nor \"Verkhoshansky\"; Wiener is on 340; \"you\" address is on 339. PDF 345 / p.334 text: \"this textbook introduced the use of methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" and \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" both present. The entry now ends \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" Minor: the entry says pages 339-340 \"address the reader as 'you'\"; the \"you\" sentences are on 339 only. Not worth a fix. | none |\n| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | GO | Fix landed. All 11 Table 6.5 rows match the PDF 341 / p.330 image. I recomputed all nine non-pull ratios from the image values: full back squat 0.60/0.35 = 1.714 (+71%); half squat 0.37/0.22 = 1.682 (+68%); bench press 0.56/0.28 = 2.00 (+100%); squat clean 1.26/0.67 = 1.881 (+88%); split clean 0.77/0.55 = 1.400 (+40%); power clean 0.69/0.65 = 1.062 (+6%); deep good morning 0.52/0.35 = 1.486 (+49%); jerk 0.60/0.54 = 1.111 (+11%); split snatch 0.89/0.67 = 1.328 (+33%); clean pull 0.50/0.50 and snatch pull 0.59/0.59 = 0%. Every percentage in the entry is right. The qualifier \"in each case where there is significant eccentric action\" is verbatim on PDF 341. The arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed values\". | none |\n| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | Every quoted string (about 40) was matched programmatically against the PDF 344-347 text, then OCR-garbled words were checked on the images (\"cyclcs\", \"unscicntific\", \"enriches ft\" read \"cycles\", \"unscientific\", \"enriches it\" on PDF 345 and 346). All are verbatim, and \"is as much a matter of art\" is on PDF 344 (the text layer reads \"is ais much\"). **Voices correct:** the citation split landed; on PDF 344 it is Verkhoshansky who \"refers to articles by Horwill (1992, 1995)\", and \"Verkhoshansky cites the work of Tschiene (1991, 1992)\". The British-decline claim (\"did not gain gold medals ... before they used such concepts\"; \"started to use Matveyev's block scheme ... widely after 1980\") is Verkhoshansky's, as labelled. **Five-year-plan argument:** PDF 345 \"He makes the interesting observation\", so it is Verkhoshansky's, reported by Siff, and it is correctly placed in his paragraph. **Zheljazkov's partial agreement:** \"He agrees that models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact ... but that this happens in all countries ... always negative\" (PDF 345); the entry's \"...\" elides only \"that\". **Corrected Example:** now quotes PDF 344 verbatim, and Zheljazkov's \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" is on PDF 345. **Chronobiology:** all quotes are on PDF 346. The subsection runs to the top of PDF 347 (reference list only), so \"PDF p. 346-347\" is right. \"Confidence for this part: `untested`\" is explicit, and the frontmatter `contested` is kept, explained in the fix log and consistent with \"When NOT to apply\". No silent change. **One new unlabelled judgment:** the chronobiology paragraph says \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" The book does not say this; it is carried over from the round-1 review's wording. It is also arguable: Zheljazkov's point on the same section's pages (PDF 345-346) rests on physiology (\"restoration and supercompensation of the various metabolic, neural and motor functions of the body do not proceed concurrently and linearly\"), though he uses it to modify periodisation rather than to argue for cycling. | Replace \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" with \"Inference: this is the only passage in 6.3 that offers a biological mechanism in support of cycled training; Zheljazkov's physiological point (heterochronous restoration, PDF p. 345-346) argues for modifying periodisation, not for cycling as such.\" |\n\n## 2. Table 6.5 recomputation\n\nDone for all 9 non-zero ratios, not only 4 (see the heart-rate row above). The image values (PDF 341) equal the entry values, and the entry's percentages equal my recomputation, to the nearest whole percent.\n\n## 3. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries (5)\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification | GO | Table 6.2 image (PDF 336 / p.325): 52 kg 51.0/63.5/70.0/78.0/80.0; 75 kg 70.0/92.0/99.0/111.0/116.0; 100 kg 82.0/101.0/113.0/125.0/132.0; +110 kg 86.0/109.0/120.0/130.0/147.0. All 20 cells match. \"optimal mean training weight\" and \"upper limits of the mean intensity\" are both on the page; Dvorkin 1980 50-70% / 60-90% / 80-90%; Medvedev 1986; \"Section 3.3.9\". |\n| framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording | GO | PDF 335 / p.324 image: five zones \"50%+ (50-59%) ... 90%+ (90-100%)\"; Table 6.1 column headers \"60%-, 60%+, 70%+, 80%+, 90%+\", then Vol, NL, Iav, 1RM, Irel (so the zone I inconsistency is real); Fig 6.7 A peaks at III, B left-skewed, C right-skewed, peaks about 40 (by eye); axis zone I labelled \"50+\". The \"mistake should not be made\" and \"individual session, the week ...\" quotes are verbatim. Notation 10(60)^1, 8(70)^1, 6(80)^2 on PDF 336. The optional \"50+\" axis note from round 1 is still not applied; it is optional. |\n| claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations | GO | PDF 343 / p.332 image: \"Chernyak et al, 1979\"; opening sentence including \"as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity\"; rec. 1 verbatim; 12-15, 9-10, 6-7 weeks; 3-4, 2-3, 3-4, 1-2 weeks. Sums 36-45. Both same-page contradictions are real; `contested` is justified. |\n| case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions | GO | PDF 333 / p.322 image: Fig 6.2A 42/23/35, B 54/30/16; Fig 6.3A 15/23/37/25, B 13/21/30/36 (Vorobyev 1978); Fig 6.4 L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S (Ermakov 1974) with \"more effective than traditional schemes which gradually increase, then decrease\"; Fig 6.5 L,M,L,S,M,L,M,L, caption \"more effective than that of Figure 6.3, since world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads\". All match. |\n| heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume | GO | PDF 329 / p.318 text: the whole quoted paragraph is verbatim through \"...heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading.\" The Inference 15/37 = 40.5% matches the Fig 6.3A image values. |\n\n## 4. Mechanical checks, all 28 files\n\n- Six section headers (`**What it is** —` ... `**Related** —`): each present exactly once in all 28 files.\n- Em dashes outside section headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Wikilinks: 165 links, all resolve (staging library or vault `Knowledge/library`, matched by path suffix). The new links from the fix round (`principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm`, `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice`, `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training`, `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates`, Evans `bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` and `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`) resolve.\n- Review-history wording: none. The grep hits are false positives: \"linguistically corrected meanings\" is a source quote (training-cycle entry, PDF 327), and \"fixed\" is ordinary usage (\"fixed contract\", \"fixed original input\", \"holding volume fixed\").\n- Fix log versus files: every claim in `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md` matches the files. Nothing is overstated.\n\n## Counts\n\nGO 27, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fix (one sentence):\n1. `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md`, chronobiology paragraph: label as Inference, and qualify, the unlabelled sentence \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" (replacement text in the table above).\n\nAfter that edit the batch can be promoted without a further review round. A grep check is enough to confirm it.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":585,"cache_read_input_tokens":134887,"output_tokens":5286,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":705},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":585},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5286,"cache_read_input_tokens":134887,"cache_creation_input_tokens":585,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":585,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XNjetdCjjv7ja6VvthY87A":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 6a (6.1-6.3), 28 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11. Text pulled with PyMuPDF; images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_6a_p<N>.png` (plus crops `r2_6a_p345crop.png`, `r2_6a_p346crop.png`). Images opened: PDF 333 (Figs 6.2-6.5), 335 (Fig 6.7, Table 6.1), 336 (Table 6.2, notations), 341 (Table 6.5), 343 (Chernyak), and the PDF 345 and 346 crops (OCR-garbled quote words). Text checked for PDF 325, 329, 334, 339, 340, 344, 345, 346, 347.\nInputs read: `review_book_prompt.md`, `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, round-1 review `review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md`, fix log `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md`.\n\n## 1. Round-1 FIX entries (6)\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson | GO | Fix landed: \"warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\". PDF 325 / p.314 text: Dyson's five-phase list, then the heading \"Ways of Organising Training\", then \"may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation ... This is far from the truth\". Dyson 5 mo / 1 mo / 6 / 6 / 6 wk and \"gym training and cross-country running\" rechecked on PDF 325. No new error. | none |\n| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability | GO | Fix landed: \"the second bullet in the source list ('the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification', PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318)\". PDF 329 bullet order confirmed: (1) eliminating disruptive factors, (2) premature introduction of skills, (3) mental control. Quote verbatim. Labelled Inference. 70-80% and \"zone-by-zone mastering of intensity (Matveyev, 1977)\" rechecked. | none |\n| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | GO | Fix landed and correct. PDF 334 / p.323: Chernyak (1978) \">40% of 1RM\"; \"warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included\"; Exercise A 60x10, 80x8, 100x4, 110x2, 1RM 120; \"The first set with 60kg (i.e. 50% of 1RM) is ignored\"; Va = 640+400+220 = 1260; Ra = 14; Ia = 90; Irel = 75%. 80/120 = 66.7%, so the example follows neither rule; the arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed numbers\". The separate 62.5% printing-slip Inference (3x3 at 75 kg) is still correct. No new error. | none |\n| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | GO | Fix landed. PyMuPDF text of PDF 339 and 340 contains neither \"Siff\" nor \"Verkhoshansky\"; Wiener is on 340; \"you\" address is on 339. PDF 345 / p.334 text: \"this textbook introduced the use of methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" and \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" both present. The entry now ends \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" Minor: the entry says pages 339-340 \"address the reader as 'you'\"; the \"you\" sentences are on 339 only. Not worth a fix. | none |\n| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | GO | Fix landed. All 11 Table 6.5 rows match the PDF 341 / p.330 image. I recomputed all nine non-pull ratios from the image values: full back squat 0.60/0.35 = 1.714 (+71%); half squat 0.37/0.22 = 1.682 (+68%); bench press 0.56/0.28 = 2.00 (+100%); squat clean 1.26/0.67 = 1.881 (+88%); split clean 0.77/0.55 = 1.400 (+40%); power clean 0.69/0.65 = 1.062 (+6%); deep good morning 0.52/0.35 = 1.486 (+49%); jerk 0.60/0.54 = 1.111 (+11%); split snatch 0.89/0.67 = 1.328 (+33%); clean pull 0.50/0.50 and snatch pull 0.59/0.59 = 0%. Every percentage in the entry is right. The qualifier \"in each case where there is significant eccentric action\" is verbatim on PDF 341. The arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed values\". | none |\n| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | Every quoted string (about 40) was matched programmatically against the PDF 344-347 text, then OCR-garbled words were checked on the images (\"cyclcs\", \"unscicntific\", \"enriches ft\" read \"cycles\", \"unscientific\", \"enriches it\" on PDF 345 and 346). All are verbatim, and \"is as much a matter of art\" is on PDF 344 (the text layer reads \"is ais much\"). **Voices correct:** the citation split landed; on PDF 344 it is Verkhoshansky who \"refers to articles by Horwill (1992, 1995)\", and \"Verkhoshansky cites the work of Tschiene (1991, 1992)\". The British-decline claim (\"did not gain gold medals ... before they used such concepts\"; \"started to use Matveyev's block scheme ... widely after 1980\") is Verkhoshansky's, as labelled. **Five-year-plan argument:** PDF 345 \"He makes the interesting observation\", so it is Verkhoshansky's, reported by Siff, and it is correctly placed in his paragraph. **Zheljazkov's partial agreement:** \"He agrees that models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact ... but that this happens in all countries ... always negative\" (PDF 345); the entry's \"...\" elides only \"that\". **Corrected Example:** now quotes PDF 344 verbatim, and Zheljazkov's \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" is on PDF 345. **Chronobiology:** all quotes are on PDF 346. The subsection runs to the top of PDF 347 (reference list only), so \"PDF p. 346-347\" is right. \"Confidence for this part: `untested`\" is explicit, and the frontmatter `contested` is kept, explained in the fix log and consistent with \"When NOT to apply\". No silent change. **One new unlabelled judgment:** the chronobiology paragraph says \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" The book does not say this; it is carried over from the round-1 review's wording. It is also arguable: Zheljazkov's point on the same section's pages (PDF 345-346) rests on physiology (\"restoration and supercompensation of the various metabolic, neural and motor functions of the body do not proceed concurrently and linearly\"), though he uses it to modify periodisation rather than to argue for cycling. | Replace \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" with \"Inference: this is the only passage in 6.3 that offers a biological mechanism in support of cycled training; Zheljazkov's physiological point (heterochronous restoration, PDF p. 345-346) argues for modifying periodisation, not for cycling as such.\" |\n\n## 2. Table 6.5 recomputation\n\nDone for all 9 non-zero ratios, not only 4 (see the heart-rate row above). The image values (PDF 341) equal the entry values, and the entry's percentages equal my recomputation, to the nearest whole percent.\n\n## 3. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries (5)\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification | GO | Table 6.2 image (PDF 336 / p.325): 52 kg 51.0/63.5/70.0/78.0/80.0; 75 kg 70.0/92.0/99.0/111.0/116.0; 100 kg 82.0/101.0/113.0/125.0/132.0; +110 kg 86.0/109.0/120.0/130.0/147.0. All 20 cells match. \"optimal mean training weight\" and \"upper limits of the mean intensity\" are both on the page; Dvorkin 1980 50-70% / 60-90% / 80-90%; Medvedev 1986; \"Section 3.3.9\". |\n| framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording | GO | PDF 335 / p.324 image: five zones \"50%+ (50-59%) ... 90%+ (90-100%)\"; Table 6.1 column headers \"60%-, 60%+, 70%+, 80%+, 90%+\", then Vol, NL, Iav, 1RM, Irel (so the zone I inconsistency is real); Fig 6.7 A peaks at III, B left-skewed, C right-skewed, peaks about 40 (by eye); axis zone I labelled \"50+\". The \"mistake should not be made\" and \"individual session, the week ...\" quotes are verbatim. Notation 10(60)^1, 8(70)^1, 6(80)^2 on PDF 336. The optional \"50+\" axis note from round 1 is still not applied; it is optional. |\n| claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations | GO | PDF 343 / p.332 image: \"Chernyak et al, 1979\"; opening sentence including \"as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity\"; rec. 1 verbatim; 12-15, 9-10, 6-7 weeks; 3-4, 2-3, 3-4, 1-2 weeks. Sums 36-45. Both same-page contradictions are real; `contested` is justified. |\n| case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions | GO | PDF 333 / p.322 image: Fig 6.2A 42/23/35, B 54/30/16; Fig 6.3A 15/23/37/25, B 13/21/30/36 (Vorobyev 1978); Fig 6.4 L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S (Ermakov 1974) with \"more effective than traditional schemes which gradually increase, then decrease\"; Fig 6.5 L,M,L,S,M,L,M,L, caption \"more effective than that of Figure 6.3, since world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads\". All match. |\n| heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume | GO | PDF 329 / p.318 text: the whole quoted paragraph is verbatim through \"...heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading.\" The Inference 15/37 = 40.5% matches the Fig 6.3A image values. |\n\n## 4. Mechanical checks, all 28 files\n\n- Six section headers (`**What it is** —` ... `**Related** —`): each present exactly once in all 28 files.\n- Em dashes outside section headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Wikilinks: 165 links, all resolve (staging library or vault `Knowledge/library`, matched by path suffix). The new links from the fix round (`principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm`, `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice`, `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training`, `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates`, Evans `bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` and `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`) resolve.\n- Review-history wording: none. The grep hits are false positives: \"linguistically corrected meanings\" is a source quote (training-cycle entry, PDF 327), and \"fixed\" is ordinary usage (\"fixed contract\", \"fixed original input\", \"holding volume fixed\").\n- Fix log versus files: every claim in `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md` matches the files. Nothing is overstated.\n\n## Counts\n\nGO 27, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fix (one sentence):\n1. `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md`, chronobiology paragraph: label as Inference, and qualify, the unlabelled sentence \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" (replacement text in the table above).\n\nAfter that edit the batch can be promoted without a further review round. A grep check is enough to confirm it.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffo64y9DhMaZrsCuCzHi","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"89de4f67-9380-470a-925d-85b58edd061b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:43:08.830Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"89de4f67-9380-470a-925d-85b58edd061b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"aff5f77e5ff2eecdd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XNjetdCjjv7ja6VvthY87A","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus_round2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e5f8f4f6-5a06-4b61-a869-1162511c7572","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:43:08.929Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 6a (6.1-6.3), 28 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11. Text pulled with PyMuPDF; images rendered at 200 dpi to `staging/_renders/r2_6a_p<N>.png` (plus crops `r2_6a_p345crop.png`, `r2_6a_p346crop.png`). Images opened: PDF 333 (Figs 6.2-6.5), 335 (Fig 6.7, Table 6.1), 336 (Table 6.2, notations), 341 (Table 6.5), 343 (Chernyak), and the PDF 345 and 346 crops (OCR-garbled quote words). Text checked for PDF 325, 329, 334, 339, 340, 344, 345, 346, 347.\nInputs read: `review_book_prompt.md`, `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, round-1 review `review_supertraining_ch6a_opus.md`, fix log `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md`.\n\n## 1. Round-1 FIX entries (6)\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--early-history-of-periodisation-kotov-to-dyson | GO | Fix landed: \"warn on the same page, directly after Dyson's five phases\". PDF 325 / p.314 text: Dyson's five-phase list, then the heading \"Ways of Organising Training\", then \"may create the impression that periodisation is the only type of organisation ... This is far from the truth\". Dyson 5 mo / 1 mo / 6 / 6 / 6 wk and \"gym training and cross-country running\" rechecked on PDF 325. No new error. | none |\n| heuristic--progress-skills-only-at-70-80-percent-technical-reliability | GO | Fix landed: \"the second bullet in the source list ('the premature introduction of skills for an athlete of a certain qualification', PDF p. 329 / printed p. 318)\". PDF 329 bullet order confirmed: (1) eliminating disruptive factors, (2) premature introduction of skills, (3) mental control. Quote verbatim. Labelled Inference. 70-80% and \"zone-by-zone mastering of intensity (Matveyev, 1977)\" rechecked. | none |\n| framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | GO | Fix landed and correct. PDF 334 / p.323: Chernyak (1978) \">40% of 1RM\"; \"warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included\"; Exercise A 60x10, 80x8, 100x4, 110x2, 1RM 120; \"The first set with 60kg (i.e. 50% of 1RM) is ignored\"; Va = 640+400+220 = 1260; Ra = 14; Ia = 90; Irel = 75%. 80/120 = 66.7%, so the example follows neither rule; the arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed numbers\". The separate 62.5% printing-slip Inference (3x3 at 75 kg) is still correct. No new error. | none |\n| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | GO | Fix landed. PyMuPDF text of PDF 339 and 340 contains neither \"Siff\" nor \"Verkhoshansky\"; Wiener is on 340; \"you\" address is on 339. PDF 345 / p.334 text: \"this textbook introduced the use of methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" and \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" both present. The entry now ends \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" Minor: the entry says pages 339-340 \"address the reader as 'you'\"; the \"you\" sentences are on 339 only. Not worth a fix. | none |\n| heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | GO | Fix landed. All 11 Table 6.5 rows match the PDF 341 / p.330 image. I recomputed all nine non-pull ratios from the image values: full back squat 0.60/0.35 = 1.714 (+71%); half squat 0.37/0.22 = 1.682 (+68%); bench press 0.56/0.28 = 2.00 (+100%); squat clean 1.26/0.67 = 1.881 (+88%); split clean 0.77/0.55 = 1.400 (+40%); power clean 0.69/0.65 = 1.062 (+6%); deep good morning 0.52/0.35 = 1.486 (+49%); jerk 0.60/0.54 = 1.111 (+11%); split snatch 0.89/0.67 = 1.328 (+33%); clean pull 0.50/0.50 and snatch pull 0.59/0.59 = 0%. Every percentage in the entry is right. The qualifier \"in each case where there is significant eccentric action\" is verbatim on PDF 341. The arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed values\". | none |\n| claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | Every quoted string (about 40) was matched programmatically against the PDF 344-347 text, then OCR-garbled words were checked on the images (\"cyclcs\", \"unscicntific\", \"enriches ft\" read \"cycles\", \"unscientific\", \"enriches it\" on PDF 345 and 346). All are verbatim, and \"is as much a matter of art\" is on PDF 344 (the text layer reads \"is ais much\"). **Voices correct:** the citation split landed; on PDF 344 it is Verkhoshansky who \"refers to articles by Horwill (1992, 1995)\", and \"Verkhoshansky cites the work of Tschiene (1991, 1992)\". The British-decline claim (\"did not gain gold medals ... before they used such concepts\"; \"started to use Matveyev's block scheme ... widely after 1980\") is Verkhoshansky's, as labelled. **Five-year-plan argument:** PDF 345 \"He makes the interesting observation\", so it is Verkhoshansky's, reported by Siff, and it is correctly placed in his paragraph. **Zheljazkov's partial agreement:** \"He agrees that models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact ... but that this happens in all countries ... always negative\" (PDF 345); the entry's \"...\" elides only \"that\". **Corrected Example:** now quotes PDF 344 verbatim, and Zheljazkov's \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" is on PDF 345. **Chronobiology:** all quotes are on PDF 346. The subsection runs to the top of PDF 347 (reference list only), so \"PDF p. 346-347\" is right. \"Confidence for this part: `untested`\" is explicit, and the frontmatter `contested` is kept, explained in the fix log and consistent with \"When NOT to apply\". No silent change. **One new unlabelled judgment:** the chronobiology paragraph says \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" The book does not say this; it is carried over from the round-1 review's wording. It is also arguable: Zheljazkov's point on the same section's pages (PDF 345-346) rests on physiology (\"restoration and supercompensation of the various metabolic, neural and motor functions of the body do not proceed concurrently and linearly\"), though he uses it to modify periodisation rather than to argue for cycling. | Replace \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" with \"Inference: this is the only passage in 6.3 that offers a biological mechanism in support of cycled training; Zheljazkov's physiological point (heterochronous restoration, PDF p. 345-346) argues for modifying periodisation, not for cycling as such.\" |\n\n## 2. Table 6.5 recomputation\n\nDone for all 9 non-zero ratios, not only 4 (see the heart-rate row above). The image values (PDF 341) equal the entry values, and the entry's percentages equal my recomputation, to the nearest whole percent.\n\n## 3. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries (5)\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--vorobyev-mean-training-weight-by-bodymass-and-qualification | GO | Table 6.2 image (PDF 336 / p.325): 52 kg 51.0/63.5/70.0/78.0/80.0; 75 kg 70.0/92.0/99.0/111.0/116.0; 100 kg 82.0/101.0/113.0/125.0/132.0; +110 kg 86.0/109.0/120.0/130.0/147.0. All 20 cells match. \"optimal mean training weight\" and \"upper limits of the mean intensity\" are both on the page; Dvorkin 1980 50-70% / 60-90% / 80-90%; Medvedev 1986; \"Section 3.3.9\". |\n| framework--intensity-zone-distribution-and-load-recording | GO | PDF 335 / p.324 image: five zones \"50%+ (50-59%) ... 90%+ (90-100%)\"; Table 6.1 column headers \"60%-, 60%+, 70%+, 80%+, 90%+\", then Vol, NL, Iav, 1RM, Irel (so the zone I inconsistency is real); Fig 6.7 A peaks at III, B left-skewed, C right-skewed, peaks about 40 (by eye); axis zone I labelled \"50+\". The \"mistake should not be made\" and \"individual session, the week ...\" quotes are verbatim. Notation 10(60)^1, 8(70)^1, 6(80)^2 on PDF 336. The optional \"50+\" axis note from round 1 is still not applied; it is optional. |\n| claim--chernyak-volume-intensity-week-combinations | GO | PDF 343 / p.332 image: \"Chernyak et al, 1979\"; opening sentence including \"as well as weeks with high volume and high intensity\"; rec. 1 verbatim; 12-15, 9-10, 6-7 weeks; 3-4, 2-3, 3-4, 1-2 weeks. Sums 36-45. Both same-page contradictions are real; `contested` is justified. |\n| case-study--vorobyev-ermakov-weightlifting-load-distributions | GO | PDF 333 / p.322 image: Fig 6.2A 42/23/35, B 54/30/16; Fig 6.3A 15/23/37/25, B 13/21/30/36 (Vorobyev 1978); Fig 6.4 L,M,L,S,L,M,ML,S (Ermakov 1974) with \"more effective than traditional schemes which gradually increase, then decrease\"; Fig 6.5 L,M,L,S,M,L,M,L, caption \"more effective than that of Figure 6.3, since world-class competitors can tolerate significantly larger loads\". All match. |\n| heuristic--rule-of-sixty-percent-light-day-volume | GO | PDF 329 / p.318 text: the whole quoted paragraph is verbatim through \"...heavy (maximal or near-maximal) loading.\" The Inference 15/37 = 40.5% matches the Fig 6.3A image values. |\n\n## 4. Mechanical checks, all 28 files\n\n- Six section headers (`**What it is** —` ... `**Related** —`): each present exactly once in all 28 files.\n- Em dashes outside section headers: none.\n- Wrapped wikilinks: none.\n- Wikilinks: 165 links, all resolve (staging library or vault `Knowledge/library`, matched by path suffix). The new links from the fix round (`principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm`, `principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice`, `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training`, `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates`, Evans `bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types` and `ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan`) resolve.\n- Review-history wording: none. The grep hits are false positives: \"linguistically corrected meanings\" is a source quote (training-cycle entry, PDF 327), and \"fixed\" is ordinary usage (\"fixed contract\", \"fixed original input\", \"holding volume fixed\").\n- Fix log versus files: every claim in `fixes_supertraining_ch6a_applied.md` matches the files. Nothing is overstated.\n\n## Counts\n\nGO 27, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fix (one sentence):\n1. `claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md`, chronobiology paragraph: label as Inference, and qualify, the unlabelled sentence \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" (replacement text in the table above).\n\nAfter that edit the batch can be promoted without a further review round. A grep check is enough to confirm it.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"89de4f67-9380-470a-925d-85b58edd061b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
