ing of intensity (Matveyev, 1977)\" rechecked. | none |"," | framework--volume-intensity-and-relative-intensity-calculation | GO | Fix landed and correct. PDF 334 / p.323: Chernyak (1978) \">40% of 1RM\"; \"warmup sets with loads of less than about 70% of 1RM are not included\"; Exercise A 60x10, 80x8, 100x4, 110x2, 1RM 120; \"The first set with 60kg (i.e. 50% of 1RM) is ignored\"; Va = 640+400+220 = 1260; Ra = 14; Ia = 90; Irel = 75%. 80/120 = 66.7%, so the example follows neither rule; the arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed numbers\". The separate 62.5% printing-slip Inference (3x3 at 75 kg) is still correct. No new error. | none |","-| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | GO | Fix landed. PyMuPDF text of PDF 339 and 340 contains neither \"Siff\" nor \"Verkhoshansky\"; Wiener is on 340; \"you\" address is on 339. PDF 345 / p.334 text: \"this textbook introduced the use of methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" and \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" both present. The entry now ends \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" Minor: the entry says pages 339-340 \"address the reader as 'you'\"; the \"you\" sentences are on 339 only. Not worth a fix. | none |","+| framework--cybernetic-periodisation-rpe-and-rating-of-technique | GO | Fix landed. PyMuPDF text of PDF 339 and 340 contains neither \"Siff\" nor \"Verkhoshansky\"; Wiener is on 340; \"you\" address is on 339. PDF 345 / p.334 text: \"this textbook introduced the use of methods based upon ongoing subjective and objective assessment\" and \"(see the section on Cybernetic Periodisation)\" both present. The entry now ends \"Inference: possibly Siff's contribution, but the page does not say so; treat it as the authors'.\" Minor: the entry says pages 339-340 \"address the reader as 'you'\"; a case-insensitive search finds \"you\" on 339 only, none on 340. Not worth a fix. | none |"," | heuristic--heart-rate-work-capacity-tests-for-lifters | GO | Fix landed. All 11 Table 6.5 rows match the PDF 341 / p.330 image. I recomputed all nine non-pull ratios from the image values: full back squat 0.60/0.35 = 1.714 (+71%); half squat 0.37/0.22 = 1.682 (+68%); bench press 0.56/0.28 = 2.00 (+100%); squat clean 1.26/0.67 = 1.881 (+88%); split clean 0.77/0.55 = 1.400 (+40%); power clean 0.69/0.65 = 1.062 (+6%); deep good morning 0.52/0.35 = 1.486 (+49%); jerk 0.60/0.54 = 1.111 (+11%); split snatch 0.89/0.67 = 1.328 (+33%); clean pull 0.50/0.50 and snatch pull 0.59/0.59 = 0%. Every percentage in the entry is right. The qualifier \"in each case where there is significant eccentric action\" is verbatim on PDF 341. The arithmetic is labelled \"Inference from the printed values\". | none |"," | claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov | FIX | Every quoted string (about 40) was matched programmatically against the PDF 344-347 text, then OCR-garbled words were checked on the images (\"cyclcs\", \"unscicntific\", \"enriches ft\" read \"cycles\", \"unscientific\", \"enriches it\" on PDF 345 and 346). All are verbatim, and \"is as much a matter of art\" is on PDF 344 (the text layer reads \"is ais much\"). **Voices correct:** the citation split landed; on PDF 344 it is Verkhoshansky who \"refers to articles by Horwill (1992, 1995)\", and \"Verkhoshansky cites the work of Tschiene (1991, 1992)\". The British-decline claim (\"did not gain gold medals ... before they used such concepts\"; \"started to use Matveyev's block scheme ... widely after 1980\") is Verkhoshansky's, as labelled. **Five-year-plan argument:** PDF 345 \"He makes the interesting observation\", so it is Verkhoshansky's, reported by Siff, and it is correctly placed in his paragraph. **Zheljazkov's partial agreement:** \"He agrees that models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact ... but that this happens in all countries ... always negative\" (PDF 345); the entry's \"...\" elides only \"that\". **Corrected Example:** now quotes PDF 344 verbatim, and Zheljazkov's \"all sports in all nations naturally undergo periods of ascendancy and decline\" is on PDF 345. **Chronobiology:** all quotes are on PDF 346. The subsection runs to the top of PDF 347 (reference list only), so \"PDF p. 346-347\" is right. \"Confidence for this part: `untested`\" is explicit, and the frontmatter `contested` is kept, explained in the fix log and consistent with \"When NOT to apply\". No silent change. **One new unlabelled judgment:** the chronobiology paragraph says \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" The book does not say this; it is carried over from the round-1 review's wording. It is also arguable: Zheljazkov's point on the same section's pages (PDF 345-346) rests on physiology (\"restoration and supercompensation of the various metabolic, neural and motor functions of the body do not proceed concurrently and linearly\"), though he uses it to modify periodisation rather than to argue for cycling. | Replace \"This is the section's only physiological argument for cycled training.\" with \"Inference: this is the only passage in 6.3 that offers a biological mechanism in support of cycled training; Zheljazkov's physiological point (heterochronous restoration, PDF p. 345-346) argues for modifying periodisation, not for cycling as such.\" |"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b0949031-6cac-4720-a9bb-e1cdb48c1afa","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
